  { id: "de-hamelin-pied-piper-house", country: "de", name: "Pied Piper's House, Hamelin", name_zh: "哈默尔恩 · 捕鼠人之家", lat: 52.1042, lon: 9.3607, place_type: "original", note: "传说 1284 年，一个穿彩衣的吹笛人吹着笛子把哈默尔恩的老鼠引进威悉河淹死，市民却赖掉了工钱，他就用笛声把全城一百三十个孩子带走，再也没有回来。格林兄弟收进了《德国传说》，勃朗宁写成英语世界人人会背的童诗。镇上这座文艺复兴时期的“捕鼠人之家”外墙刻着这段记载，旁边那条小巷至今不许奏乐，夏天的星期天广场上还会演这出戏", note_en: "Legend says that in 1284 a piper in a coat of many colors piped the rats of Hamelin into the Weser to drown, and when the townspeople refused to pay him he piped away a hundred and thirty of their children, who were never seen again. The Brothers Grimm included it in their German Legends, and Robert Browning turned it into the children's poem every English speaker knows. An inscription on this Renaissance house, the Rattenfängerhaus, records the story; the lane beside it is still a street where no music may be played, and on summer Sundays the tale is acted out in the square", coord_checked: "Rattenfängerhaus Hameln (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Rattenfängerhaus_Hameln", book_ids: ["pied-piper-of-hamelin"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pied_Piper_of_Hamelin", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-hamelin-pied-piper-house.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 1067, "author": "Bush6984", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Pied_Piper%27s_House_%28Rattenf%C3%A4ngerhaus%29_in_Hameln.JPG", "alt_en": "The Pied Piper's House in Hamelin", "alt_zh": "哈默尔恩的捕鼠人之家"} },
  { id: "de-bodenwerder-munchhausen", country: "de", name: "Münchhausen's birthplace, Bodenwerder", name_zh: "博登韦尔德 · 闵希豪森故居", lat: 51.9761, lon: 9.5134, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "真实的闵希豪森男爵 1720 年生在威悉河边这座庄园里，在俄国军队里打过仗，退役回乡后最爱在饭桌上讲自己的离奇经历：骑着炮弹飞、拽着自己的辫子把自己和马拉出沼泽、马被城门劈成两半还照样喝水。1785 年拉斯伯把这些故事用英文出版，男爵从此成了“吹牛大王”，他本人却很生气。庄园现在是市政厅，里面有闵希豪森博物馆，花园里有他骑着半匹马喝水的喷泉", note_en: "The real Baron Münchhausen was born in 1720 in this manor house on the Weser, fought in the Russian army, and back home loved to tell outrageous stories at dinner: riding a cannonball, pulling himself and his horse out of a bog by his own pigtail, his horse cut in half by a falling portcullis and still drinking. In 1785 Rudolf Erich Raspe published the tales in English, and the baron became the world's most famous liar, much to his annoyance. The manor is now the town hall, with a Münchhausen museum inside and a fountain in the garden showing him on half a horse", coord_checked: "Schloss Münchhausen (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Schloss_Münchhausen", book_ids: ["baron-munchausen"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hieronymus_Karl_Friedrich,_Freiherr_von_M%C3%BCnchhausen", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-bodenwerder-munchhausen.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 500, "author": "JoachimKohler-HB", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ABodenwerder_-_M%C3%BCnchhausens_Geburtshaus.jpg", "alt_en": "Münchhausen's birthplace, now the town hall of Bodenwerder", "alt_zh": "闵希豪森的出生地，今天的博登韦尔德市政厅"} },
  { id: "de-bremen-town-musicians", country: "de", name: "Town Musicians statue, Bremen", name_zh: "不来梅 · 不来梅的城市乐手雕像", lat: 53.0761, lon: 8.8072, place_type: "original", note: "格林童话《不来梅的城市乐手》里，一头老驴、一条老狗、一只老猫和一只公鸡被主人嫌弃，结伴去不来梅当城市乐手，路上用叠罗汉吓跑了强盗——有意思的是，它们最后根本没走到不来梅。市政厅西侧这组叠在一起的铜像是 1953 年格哈德·马尔克斯做的，据说两手握住驴的前腿许愿就能实现，所以驴腿被摸得锃亮", note_en: "In the Grimms' tale of the Bremen Town Musicians, an old donkey, dog, cat and rooster, all unwanted by their masters, set off together to become town musicians in Bremen and on the way scare off a band of robbers by climbing on each other's backs; the funny thing is that they never actually get to Bremen. This bronze stack of animals beside the town hall was made by Gerhard Marcks in 1953, and holding the donkey's front legs is supposed to make your wish come true, which is why they're polished to a shine", coord_checked: "Die Bremer Stadtmusikanten (Gerhard Marcks) (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Die_Bremer_Stadtmusikanten_(Gerhard_Marcks)", book_ids: ["grimms-fairy-tales"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Town_Musicians_of_Bremen", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-bremen-town-musicians.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "Jorge Franganillo", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ABremen_-_Die_Bremer_Stadtmusikanten.jpg", "alt_en": "The Town Musicians of Bremen by Gerhard Marcks", "alt_zh": "马尔克斯的不来梅城市乐手雕像"} },
  { id: "de-sababurg", country: "de", name: "Sababurg", name_zh: "萨巴堡", lat: 51.5436, lon: 9.5375, place_type: "prototype", prototype_of: "格林童话《睡美人》（《荆棘公主》）里那座被荆棘围住、一睡百年的城堡，在德国童话之路上就被认作是莱因哈特森林深处的这座萨巴堡。格林兄弟在附近的卡塞尔收集童话，城堡建于 14 世纪，后来荒废，四周确实长满了玫瑰和荆棘。现在是一家城堡酒店，旁边就是德国最古老的原始森林保护区之一", prototype_of_en: "The thorn-wrapped castle where Sleeping Beauty (Little Briar-Rose, in the Grimms' version) sleeps for a hundred years is, on Germany's Fairy Tale Route, this castle deep in the Reinhardswald. The Grimms collected their tales in nearby Kassel, and the fourteenth-century castle, long a ruin, really was overgrown with roses and briars. It's now a castle hotel, next to one of the oldest primeval-forest reserves in Germany", coord_checked: "Sababurg (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Sababurg", book_ids: ["grimms-fairy-tales"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sababurg", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-sababurg.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Kersti", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A087_Vor_den_verschlossenen_Toren_der_Sababurg.jpg", "alt_en": "The gate of the Sababurg", "alt_zh": "萨巴堡的城门"} },
  { id: "de-goethe-house-frankfurt", country: "de", name: "Goethe House, Frankfurt", name_zh: "法兰克福 · 歌德故居", lat: 50.1112, lon: 8.6777, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "歌德 1749 年 8 月 28 日正午出生在这座四层楼的房子里，他在自传《诗与真》开头就这样写，连当时的星象都记了下来。他在这里长大，小时候得到的木偶戏台后来成了《威廉·迈斯特》的灵感，《少年维特之烦恼》和《浮士德》的最初稿子也是在这里写的。房子在二战中被炸毁，战后按原样重建，祖母送的木偶戏台还在", note_en: "Goethe was born in this four-story house at noon on 28 August 1749, as he tells us at the very start of his autobiography, Poetry and Truth, down to the position of the planets. He grew up here; the puppet theater he was given as a child later inspired Wilhelm Meister, and he wrote The Sorrows of Young Werther and the first drafts of Faust here. The house was bombed in the Second World War and rebuilt exactly as it was, and the puppet theater from his grandmother is still there", coord_checked: "Goethehaus (Frankfurt) (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Goethehaus_(Frankfurt)", book_ids: ["poetry-and-truth", "faust", "the-sorrows-of-young-werther"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goethe_House", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-goethe-house-frankfurt.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Munin2005", "license": "CC BY 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AGiebel_des_Goethe-Hauses_in_Frankfurt_am_Main.JPG", "alt_en": "Goethe's birthplace in Frankfurt", "alt_zh": "法兰克福的歌德出生地"} },
  { id: "de-goethe-house-weimar", country: "de", name: "Goethe's House on the Frauenplan, Weimar", name_zh: "魏玛 · 歌德故居", lat: 50.9775, lon: 11.3286, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "歌德从 1782 年起住在魏玛这座黄色大宅里，直到 1832 年去世，《亲和力》和《浮士德》第二部都是住在这里时写成的。书房、藏书室和他临终坐着的扶手椅都原样保留，几分钟路外就是席勒故居", note_en: "Goethe lived in this big yellow house in Weimar from 1782 until his death in 1832, and wrote Elective Affinities and the second part of Faust while living here. His study, his library and the armchair he died in are kept as they were, and Schiller's house is a few minutes' walk away", coord_checked: "Goethes Wohnhaus (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Goethes_Wohnhaus", book_ids: ["faust", "elective-affinities"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goethe%27s_house_(Weimar)", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-goethe-house-weimar.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Bewahrerderwerte", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AWeimar_-_Goethe_House_-_20200911094254.jpg", "alt_en": "Goethe's house on the Frauenplan, Weimar", "alt_zh": "魏玛弗劳恩广场边的歌德故居"} },
  { id: "de-auerbachs-keller", country: "de", name: "Auerbachs Keller, Leipzig", name_zh: "莱比锡 · 奥尔巴赫地窖酒馆", lat: 51.3394, lon: 12.3754, place_type: "original", note: "《浮士德》第一部里，魔鬼梅菲斯特带浮士德下到这家地窖酒馆，和一群醉醺醺的大学生喝酒取乐，用魔法让桌子流出葡萄酒。歌德在莱比锡念大学时常来，酒馆里还挂着 1625 年的壁画，画的是传说中浮士德骑着酒桶飞出地窖。今天它仍是一家餐馆，梅德勒拱廊入口两边立着浮士德、梅菲斯特和被施了魔法的大学生铜像", note_en: "In Faust Part One, Mephistopheles takes Faust down into this cellar tavern to drink with a crowd of tipsy students, and makes wine flow from the table by magic. Goethe came here often when he was a student in Leipzig, and the tavern still has paintings from 1625 showing the legendary Faust riding out of the cellar on a barrel. It's still a restaurant, and bronze figures of Faust, Mephisto and the bewitched students flank its entrance in the Mädler arcade", coord_checked: "Auerbachs Keller (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Auerbachs_Keller", book_ids: ["faust"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auerbachs_Keller", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-auerbachs-keller.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 451, "author": "Rudolf.volz", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AAuerbachs-Keller-17.jpg", "alt_en": "The great hall of Auerbachs Keller", "alt_zh": "奥尔巴赫地窖酒馆的大厅"} },
  { id: "de-dresden-slaughterhouse", country: "de", name: "Slaughterhouse-Five, Dresden", name_zh: "德累斯顿 · 五号屠场", lat: 51.0702, lon: 13.7145, place_type: "original", note: "1945 年 2 月 13 日盟军轰炸德累斯顿时，二十二岁的美军战俘冯内古特就关在这片屠宰场里，躲在地下冻肉库里活了下来，出来时整座城已是一片焦土。二十多年后他把这段经历写进《五号屠场》，主人公比利·皮尔格林“在时间里脱了钩”。屠宰场旧址如今是德累斯顿会展中心，地下室偶尔开放参观", note_en: "When the Allies firebombed Dresden on 13 February 1945, Kurt Vonnegut, a twenty-two-year-old American prisoner of war, was being held in this slaughterhouse complex; he survived in an underground meat locker and came out to find the city burned to the ground. More than twenty years later he put the experience into Slaughterhouse-Five, whose hero Billy Pilgrim has come 'unstuck in time'. The old slaughterhouse is now Dresden's trade fair grounds, and the cellar is occasionally opened for tours", coord_checked: "Städtischer Vieh- und Schlachthof (Dresden) (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Städtischer_Vieh-_und_Schlachthof_(Dresden)", book_ids: ["slaughterhouse-five"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slaughterhouse-Five", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-dresden-slaughterhouse.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 385, "author": ".mw-parser-output .commons-creator-table{background-color:#f0f0ff;box-sizing:border-box;font-size:95%;text-align:start;c", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A20190320255MDR_Dresden-Friedrichstadt_Erlwein-Schlachthof_Zur_Messe.jpg", "alt_en": "The old municipal slaughterhouse, now the Dresden trade fair", "alt_zh": "旧市立屠宰场，今天的德累斯顿会展中心"} },
  { id: "de-nollendorfplatz", country: "de", name: "Nollendorfplatz, Berlin", name_zh: "柏林 · 诺伦多夫广场", lat: 52.4992, lon: 13.3539, place_type: "original", note: "1929 到 1933 年，英国作家衣修伍德住在广场旁边的诺伦多夫街 17 号，把魏玛末年夜夜笙歌、纳粹步步逼近的柏林写成了《别了，柏林》，后来改编成音乐剧和电影《歌厅》。楼上有一块纪念牌。同一时期凯斯特纳的《埃米尔擒贼记》里，偷了埃米尔钱的小偷也住进了这个广场边的旅馆，一大群柏林孩子在外面盯梢", note_en: "From 1929 to 1933 the English writer Christopher Isherwood lived just off this square at Nollendorfstraße 17, and turned the Berlin of the last Weimar years, all-night cabarets with the Nazis closing in, into Goodbye to Berlin, which became the musical and film Cabaret; there's a plaque on the building. In Erich Kästner's Emil and the Detectives from the same years, the thief who stole Emil's money also takes a room in a hotel on this square, while a crowd of Berlin kids keep watch outside", coord_checked: "Nollendorfplatz (Berlin-Schöneberg) (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Nollendorfplatz_(Berlin-Schöneberg)", book_ids: ["goodbye-to-berlin", "emil-and-the-detectives"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goodbye_to_Berlin", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-nollendorfplatz.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 450, "author": "Talmoryair", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A17_Nollendorfstra%C3%9Fe_01.JPG", "alt_en": "Nollendorfstraße 17, where Isherwood lived", "alt_zh": "衣修伍德住过的诺伦多夫街 17 号"} },
  { id: "de-alexanderplatz", country: "de", name: "Alexanderplatz, Berlin", name_zh: "柏林 · 亚历山大广场", lat: 52.5212, lon: 13.4133, place_type: "original", note: "德布林 1929 年的《柏林，亚历山大广场》用电影剪辑一样的手法，写刚出狱的搬运工弗兰茨·毕伯科普夫想在这片喧闹的工人街区重新做个好人，却一次次被卷回黑道。小说把电车、广告、屠宰场和街头小调全拼了进去，是德语现代主义小说的代表，后来法斯宾德拍成了十五小时的电视剧。今天的广场是战后重建的，世界时钟下是柏林人最常约见的地方", note_en: "Alfred Döblin's Berlin Alexanderplatz (1929) cuts between scenes like a film to follow Franz Biberkopf, a laborer just out of prison, as he tries to go straight in this noisy working-class quarter and is dragged back into crime again and again. It crams in trams, advertisements, slaughterhouses and street songs and is the great modernist novel in German; Fassbinder later made it into a fifteen-hour TV series. The square was rebuilt after the war, and the World Clock is where Berliners arrange to meet", coord_checked: "Urania-Weltzeituhr (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Urania-Weltzeituhr", book_ids: ["berlin-alexanderplatz"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berlin_Alexanderplatz", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-alexanderplatz.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": ".mw-parser-output .commons-creator-table{background-color:#f0f0ff;box-sizing:border-box;font-size:95%;text-align:start;c", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AReloj_Mundial%2C_Berl%C3%ADn%2C_Alemania%2C_2016-04-22%2C_DD_46-48_HDR.jpg", "alt_en": "The World Clock on Alexanderplatz", "alt_zh": "亚历山大广场上的世界时钟"} },
  { id: "de-fontane-neuruppin", country: "de", name: "Fontane monument, Neuruppin", name_zh: "新鲁平 · 冯塔纳纪念像", lat: 52.9214, lon: 12.8002, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "冯塔纳 1819 年出生在新鲁平的一家药房里，后来写了五卷《勃兰登堡边区漫游记》，把家乡的湖泊、庄园和小城一一写遍，晚年又写出德国最好的婚姻小说《艾菲·布里斯特》：十七岁的艾菲嫁给比她大二十多岁的男爵，一段旧情让她身败名裂。城里这座纪念像让他坐在石头上，手拿帽子和手杖，像刚走完一段路", note_en: "Theodor Fontane was born in 1819 in a pharmacy in Neuruppin; he went on to write five volumes of Wanderings through the March of Brandenburg, covering every lake, manor and small town of his home region, and late in life wrote Effi Briest, the great German novel of marriage, in which seventeen-year-old Effi marries a baron twenty years older and is ruined by an old affair. This monument shows him sitting on a rock with his hat and walking stick, as if resting halfway through a walk", coord_checked: "Theodor-Fontane-Denkmal (Neuruppin) (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Theodor-Fontane-Denkmal_(Neuruppin)", book_ids: ["effi-briest"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodor_Fontane", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-fontane-neuruppin.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Radler59 ( talk )", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ANeuruppin_Fontane_Denkmal.jpg", "alt_en": "The Fontane monument in Neuruppin", "alt_zh": "新鲁平的冯塔纳纪念像"} },
  { id: "de-fallada-carwitz", country: "de", name: "Hans Fallada House, Carwitz", name_zh: "卡尔维茨 · 汉斯·法拉达故居", lat: 53.3018, lon: 13.4449, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "1932 年的《小人物，怎么办？》写经济危机里一个小职员和妻子的挣扎，让法拉达一举成名，第二年他用稿费买下梅克伦堡湖区的这座农舍，种地养蜂，躲开纳粹柏林。战后他写出《柏林孤影》，出版前就去世了。故居是博物馆，他葬在村里", note_en: "Little Man, What Now? (1932), about a young clerk and his wife struggling through the Depression, made Fallada famous, and the next year he used the money to buy this farmhouse in the Mecklenburg lakes, where he farmed and kept bees well away from Nazi Berlin. After the war he wrote Alone in Berlin and died before it came out. The house is a museum, and he is buried in the village", coord_checked: "Hans-Fallada-Haus (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Hans-Fallada-Haus", book_ids: ["alone-in-berlin", "little-man-what-now"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hans_Fallada", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-fallada-carwitz.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Botaurus", "license": "Public domain", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AHans-Fallada-Haus-in-Carwitz-14-02-2009-46.jpg", "alt_en": "A room in the Hans Fallada House, Carwitz", "alt_zh": "卡尔维茨法拉达故居的房间"} },
  { id: "de-buddenbrookhaus", country: "de", name: "Buddenbrookhaus, Lübeck", name_zh: "吕贝克 · 布登勃洛克之家", lat: 53.8683, lon: 10.6858, place_type: "original", note: "托马斯·曼把祖父母的这座大宅原样写进《布登勃洛克一家》，开头就是 1835 年全家坐在孟街这座房子的“风景厅”里。二十多年后他又写出《魔山》，一个汉堡青年上山探病，在达沃斯的疗养院一住七年。这里现在是曼氏兄弟纪念馆，正在扩建，去之前先查开放情况", note_en: "Thomas Mann put his grandparents' house straight into Buddenbrooks, which opens in 1835 with the family gathered in the 'landscape room' of this house on the Mengstraße. Twenty-odd years later he wrote The Magic Mountain, in which a young man from Hamburg goes up to a Davos sanatorium for a visit and stays seven years. The house is a museum of Thomas and Heinrich Mann and is being expanded, so check whether it's open before you go", coord_checked: "Buddenbrookhaus (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Buddenbrookhaus", book_ids: ["buddenbrooks", "the-magic-mountain"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buddenbrookhaus", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-buddenbrookhaus.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": ".mw-parser-output .commons-creator-table{background-color:#f0f0ff;box-sizing:border-box;font-size:95%;text-align:start;c", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AL%C3%BCbeck%2C_Mengstra%C3%9Fe%2C_Buddenbrookhaus_--_2017_--_0479.jpg", "alt_en": "The Buddenbrookhaus on the Mengstraße, Lübeck", "alt_zh": "吕贝克孟街上的布登勃洛克之家"} },
  { id: "de-hamburg-rathausmarkt", country: "de", name: "Rathausmarkt, Hamburg", name_zh: "汉堡 · 市政厅广场", lat: 53.5505, lon: 9.9937, place_type: "original", note: "海涅年轻时在汉堡叔叔的银行里学过生意，爱上了堂妹，第一部诗集也在这里出版。1842 年一场大火烧掉了汉堡三分之一的老城，第二年他从巴黎回来探母，在长诗《德国，一个冬天的童话》里说重建中的汉堡像一只剪了一半毛的卷毛狗。今天的市政厅就是在火灾后的废墟上建起来的，广场边立着海涅的铜像", note_en: "As a young man Heine learned the business in his uncle's bank in Hamburg, fell in love with his cousin, and published his first collection of poems here. In 1842 a great fire destroyed a third of the old city, and when he came back from Paris the next year to see his mother he wrote, in the long poem Germany. A Winter's Tale, that half-rebuilt Hamburg looked like a half-shorn poodle. The present town hall was built on the ground cleared by the fire, and a bronze statue of Heine stands on the square", coord_checked: "Heinrich-Heine-Denkmal, Rathausmarkt (Hamburg) (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Heinrich-Heine-Denkmal,_Rathausmarkt_(Hamburg)", book_ids: ["germany-a-winters-tale"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_fire_of_Hamburg", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-hamburg-rathausmarkt.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 450, "author": "Oxfordian Kissuth", "license": "CC BY 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AHamburger_Rathaus_in_2016.jpg", "alt_en": "Hamburg's town hall on the Rathausmarkt", "alt_zh": "汉堡市政厅广场上的市政厅"} },
  { id: "de-heine-birthplace", country: "de", name: "Heine's birthplace, Düsseldorf", name_zh: "杜塞尔多夫 · 海涅出生地", lat: 51.2262, lon: 6.7747, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "海涅 1797 年出生在杜塞尔多夫老城博尔克街的这座房子里。他的《歌集》里有德国人人会唱的《罗蕾莱》，舒曼、舒伯特为他的诗谱了几百首歌。他后来流亡巴黎，再也没能回家。故居现在是一家文学书店兼咖啡馆，经常办朗读会，楼前就是老城最热闹的酒吧街", note_en: "Heinrich Heine was born in 1797 in this house on the Bolkerstraße in Düsseldorf's old town. His Book of Songs includes 'The Lorelei', which every German can sing, and Schumann and Schubert set hundreds of his poems to music. He later went into exile in Paris and never came home. The house is now a literary bookshop and café that often hosts readings, right on the old town's liveliest street of bars", coord_checked: "Heine Haus (Düsseldorf) (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Heine_Haus_(Düsseldorf)", book_ids: ["book-of-songs"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heinrich_Heine", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-heine-birthplace.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Островский Александр, Киев", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ANRW%2C_D%C3%BCsseldorf%2C_Altstadt_-_Bolkerstrasse_53.jpg", "alt_en": "Heine's birthplace at Bolkerstraße 53", "alt_zh": "博尔克街 53 号海涅出生地"} },
  { id: "de-loreley", country: "de", name: "Lorelei rock", name_zh: "罗蕾莱岩", lat: 50.1393, lon: 7.7287, place_type: "original", note: "莱茵河在这里拐了个急弯、河道最窄最深，自古是船夫的险滩。海涅的诗《罗蕾莱》说，岩顶坐着一个金发少女，一边梳头一边唱歌，船夫只顾抬头看她，就撞上了礁石。这首诗谱成歌后传遍德国，马克·吐温在《海外浪游记》里说起初受不了，后来成了他最喜欢的曲子。从对岸的圣戈阿尔看过去最好，岩顶有观景台", note_en: "Here the Rhine bends sharply and runs at its narrowest and deepest, and boatmen have always feared it. In Heine's poem 'The Lorelei', a golden-haired maiden sits on top of the rock combing her hair and singing, and the boatman, gazing up at her, sails straight into the rocks. Set to music, the poem spread all over Germany; Mark Twain says in A Tramp Abroad that he couldn't stand the tune at first and ended up liking it more than any other. The best view is from Sankt Goar across the river, and there's a lookout on top of the rock", coord_checked: "Loreley (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Loreley", book_ids: ["book-of-songs", "a-tramp-abroad"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lorelei", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-loreley.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "Carsten Steger", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AAerial_image_of_the_Lorelei.jpg", "alt_en": "The Lorelei rock above the bend in the Rhine", "alt_zh": "莱茵河急弯上的罗蕾莱岩"} },
  { id: "de-heidelberg-castle", country: "de", name: "Heidelberg Castle", name_zh: "海德堡城堡", lat: 49.4106, lon: 8.7158, place_type: "original", note: "1878 年马克·吐温在海德堡住了好几个月，就住在城堡上方的旅馆里，写下《海外浪游记》的开头几章：内卡河峡谷、半毁的红砂岩城堡、晚上像“坠落的银河”一样的城市灯火，还有满脸决斗伤疤的大学生。他甚至说海德堡的夜景让人“需要时间考虑再下结论”。从老桥上看城堡，或者坐缆车上去，都能看到他描写的那片风景", note_en: "Mark Twain spent several months in Heidelberg in 1878, staying at a hotel above the castle, and wrote the opening chapters of A Tramp Abroad here: the Neckar gorge, the half-ruined red sandstone castle, the city lights at night like 'a fallen Milky Way', and the students with their dueling scars. Heidelberg by night, he said, makes you take time before you give your verdict. Look up at the castle from the Old Bridge, or ride the funicular up, and you'll see the view he describes", coord_checked: "Heidelberg Castle (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Heidelberg_Castle", book_ids: ["a-tramp-abroad"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Tramp_Abroad", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-heidelberg-castle.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 534, "author": "Tilman2007", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AHeidelberg%2C_Alte_Br%C3%BCcke%2C_Schloss_20170601_001.jpg", "alt_en": "Heidelberg Castle above the Old Bridge", "alt_zh": "老桥上方的海德堡城堡"} },
  { id: "de-hesse-calw", country: "de", name: "Hermann Hesse Museum, Calw", name_zh: "卡尔夫 · 黑塞博物馆", lat: 48.7151, lon: 8.7377, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "黑塞 1877 年生在黑森林边的卡尔夫，后来在小说里一再把它写成“格伯斯奥”。《在轮下》里被逼进神学院的少年汉斯就来自这样一座小城；中年写的《荒原狼》则是一个分裂的知识分子在城市里游荡的自白。市集广场上有黑塞博物馆，老桥上立着他的铜像", note_en: "Hesse was born in 1877 in Calw, on the edge of the Black Forest, and kept writing about it under the name Gerbersau. Hans in Beneath the Wheel, pushed into a seminary until he breaks, comes from just such a town; Steppenwolf, written in middle age, is the confession of a split intellectual wandering a city at night. The Hesse museum is on the market square, and his bronze statue stands on the old bridge", coord_checked: "Hermann-Hesse-Museum (Calw) (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Hermann-Hesse-Museum_(Calw)", book_ids: ["beneath-the-wheel", "steppenwolf"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermann_Hesse", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-hesse-calw.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Silesia711", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AHermann-Hesse-Museum_Calw.jpg", "alt_en": "The Hermann Hesse Museum on the market square, Calw", "alt_zh": "卡尔夫市集广场上的黑塞博物馆"} },
  { id: "de-hoffmann-bamberg", country: "de", name: "E. T. A. Hoffmann House, Bamberg", name_zh: "班贝格 · E. T. A. 霍夫曼故居", lat: 49.8899, lon: 10.8909, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "霍夫曼 1809 到 1813 年在班贝格剧院当乐长，就住在剧院对面这座窄楼里，也在这里开始写怪诞故事。后来的《沙人》写一个被童年噩梦缠住、爱上自动玩偶的大学生，《胡桃夹子与老鼠国王》则成了柴可夫斯基芭蕾的原作。故居是一座小纪念馆", note_en: "Hoffmann was music director of the Bamberg theater from 1809 to 1813, lived in this narrow house opposite it, and began writing his uncanny tales here. The Sandman, which followed, is about a student haunted by a childhood nightmare who falls in love with a mechanical doll, and The Nutcracker and the Mouse King became Tchaikovsky's ballet. The house is a small museum", coord_checked: "Schillerplatz 26 (Bamberg) (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Schillerplatz_26_(Bamberg)", book_ids: ["nutcracker-and-mouse-king", "the-sandman"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E._T._A._Hoffmann", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-hoffmann-bamberg.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 530, "author": "Tilman2007", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ABamberg%2C_Schillerplatz_26%2C_20150925%2C_002.jpg", "alt_en": "E. T. A. Hoffmann's house on the Schillerplatz, Bamberg", "alt_zh": "班贝格席勒广场上的霍夫曼故居"} },
  { id: "de-brennender-berg", country: "de", name: "Burning Mountain, Dudweiler", name_zh: "达德魏勒 · 燃烧的山", lat: 49.2889, lon: 7.0536, place_type: "original", note: "1770 年，二十一岁的歌德从斯特拉斯堡出发骑马游历，来到萨尔布吕肯附近，专门去看了这座“燃烧的山”：地下的煤层自 17 世纪起就一直在燃烧，裂缝里冒着硫磺味的热气，靴底都烫脚。他在自传《诗与真》里详细记下了这次参观。两百五十年后地火还没完全熄灭，林间小径边仍有冒热气的裂缝，还立着一块纪念歌德来访的牌子", note_en: "In 1770 the twenty-one-year-old Goethe rode out from Strasbourg on a tour and came near Saarbrücken specially to see this 'burning mountain', where a coal seam underground had been on fire since the seventeenth century; sulfurous fumes rose from the cracks and the ground was hot through his boots. He describes the visit in detail in his autobiography, Poetry and Truth. Two hundred and fifty years later the fire still hasn't quite gone out: there are steaming fissures beside the woodland path, and a sign recalling Goethe's visit", coord_checked: "Brennender Berg (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Brennender_Berg", book_ids: ["poetry-and-truth"], fact_source: "https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brennender_Berg", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-brennender-berg.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 535, "author": "Flocci Nivis", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ABrennender_Berg_Fisheye.jpg", "alt_en": "The woods of the Burning Mountain", "alt_zh": "燃烧的山的林间"} },
  { id: "de-lottehaus-wetzlar", country: "de", name: "Lottehaus, Wetzlar", name_zh: "韦茨拉尔 · 绿蒂故居", lat: 50.5552, lon: 8.50405, place_type: "prototype", prototype_of: "1772 年歌德在韦茨拉尔的帝国法院实习，在舞会上认识了已经订婚的夏洛特·布夫，常来她家这座德意志骑士团的院子。两年后他把这段单恋写成《少年维特之烦恼》，维特第一次见到绿蒂，她正在这里给弟妹们切面包。院子还在，楼里是绿蒂故居纪念馆", prototype_of_en: "In 1772 Goethe was a trainee at the imperial court in Wetzlar; at a ball he met Charlotte Buff, already engaged, and kept coming to her family's home in this Teutonic Order courtyard. Two years later he turned his hopeless love into The Sorrows of Young Werther, and when Werther first sees Lotte here she is cutting bread for her little brothers and sisters. The courtyard is still there, and the house is a museum to Lotte", coord_checked: "Lottestraße 8-10 (Wetzlar) (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Lottestraße_8-10_(Wetzlar)", book_ids: ["the-sorrows-of-young-werther"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sorrows_of_Young_Werther", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-lottehaus-wetzlar.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 591, "author": "Dontworry", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ALottehaus-2011-wetzlar-063.jpg", "alt_en": "The Lottehaus in the Teutonic Order courtyard, Wetzlar", "alt_zh": "韦茨拉尔德意志骑士团院子里的绿蒂故居"} },
  { id: "de-brocken", country: "de", name: "Brocken", name_zh: "布罗肯峰", lat: 51.7991251, lon: 10.6156365, place_type: "original", note: "传说每年 4 月 30 日夜里，女巫们骑着扫帚飞上哈尔茨山的最高峰布罗肯峰狂欢。《浮士德》第一部里，梅菲斯特就带浮士德上山赶这场瓦尔普吉斯之夜；歌德自己 1777 年冬天登过这座山。今天可以坐蒸汽小火车上山，山顶常年雾气弥漫", note_en: "Legend says that on the night of 30 April the witches fly on their broomsticks to the Brocken, the highest peak in the Harz, for their revels. In Faust Part One, Mephistopheles takes Faust up the mountain for this Walpurgis Night, and Goethe himself climbed it in the winter of 1777. Today a steam train runs to the summit, which is wrapped in fog most of the year", coord_checked: "Brocken (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Brocken", book_ids: ["faust"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brocken", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-brocken.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 536, "author": "Leonhard Lenz", "license": "CC0", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ABrocken_from_Schindelstieg_Harz_2022-04-08_02.jpg", "alt_en": "The Brocken seen across the Harz forest", "alt_zh": "从哈尔茨林间远望布罗肯峰"} },
  { id: "de-stechlinsee", country: "de", name: "Großer Stechlinsee", name_zh: "大施泰希林湖", lat: 53.151388888889, lon: 13.026111111111, place_type: "original", note: "冯塔纳的最后一部小说《施泰希林》以这片湖命名：据说世界上哪里有大地震、火山爆发，这片平静的湖就会冒起水柱，有大事时还会飞出一只红公鸡。小说里的老贵族就住在湖边的庄园里，看着旧普鲁士慢慢退场。湖水清澈见底，环湖有步道，湖边的小村诺伊格洛布索有冯塔纳的纪念石", note_en: "Fontane's last novel takes its name from this lake: whenever there's an earthquake or an eruption somewhere in the world, they say, a waterspout rises from its still surface, and when something truly great happens a red rooster flies up out of it. The old nobleman of the novel lives on an estate by its shore, watching the old Prussia fade away. The water is famously clear, a footpath runs round it, and the village of Neuglobsow lies on the shore", coord_checked: "Großer Stechlinsee (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Großer_Stechlinsee", book_ids: ["the-stechlin"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Der_Stechlin", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-stechlinsee.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "MarcusFourmont", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlick_vom_Ostufer_%C3%BCber_den_Stechlinsee.jpg", "alt_en": "Looking across the Stechlinsee from the east shore", "alt_zh": "从东岸望大施泰希林湖"} },
  { id: "de-kaestner-museum", country: "de", name: "Erich Kästner Museum, Dresden", name_zh: "德累斯顿 · 埃里希·凯斯特纳博物馆", lat: 51.0638, lon: 13.7457, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "凯斯特纳 1899 年生在德累斯顿新城，小时候常到舅舅家这座别墅的花园里玩，后来在回忆录里写过。他写的孩子总是机灵又仗义：《飞翔的教室》里寄宿学校的男孩们，《两个小洛特》里在夏令营认出彼此的双胞胎姐妹。别墅现在是他的博物馆，花园墙头坐着一尊少年凯斯特纳的铜像", note_en: "Kästner was born in 1899 in Dresden's Neustadt and as a boy often played in the garden of this villa, which belonged to his uncle; he wrote about it in his memoir. His children are always quick and loyal: the boarding-school boys of The Flying Classroom, the twin sisters who recognize each other at summer camp in Lottie and Lisa. The villa is now his museum, and a bronze of the young Kästner sits on the garden wall", coord_checked: "Erich Kästner Museum (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Erich_Kästner_Museum", book_ids: ["lottie-and-lisa", "the-flying-classroom", "emil-and-the-detectives"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erich_K%C3%A4stner", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-kaestner-museum.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Paulae", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AAntonstra%C3%9Fe_1_Dresden_Kdm.JPG", "alt_en": "Villa Augustin, home of the Erich Kästner Museum", "alt_zh": "凯斯特纳博物馆所在的奥古斯丁别墅"} },
  { id: "de-worms-hagen", country: "de", name: "Hagen monument, Worms", name_zh: "沃尔姆斯 · 哈根像", lat: 49.6332, lon: 8.37698, place_type: "original", note: "《尼伯龙根之歌》里，勃艮第国王们的宫廷就在莱茵河边的沃尔姆斯：屠龙英雄齐格弗里德来这里求娶克里姆希尔德，后来被哈根刺死，哈根又把尼伯龙根宝藏沉进了莱茵河。河岸上这尊 1905 年的铜像就是哈根抛宝的那一刻，城墙边还有一座尼伯龙根博物馆", note_en: "In the Nibelungenlied, the Burgundian kings hold court at Worms on the Rhine: the dragon-slayer Siegfried comes here to win Kriemhild, is later killed by Hagen, and Hagen sinks the Nibelung treasure in the Rhine. This bronze of 1905 on the riverbank shows him at the moment he throws the hoard in, and there's a Nibelungen museum in the towers of the city wall nearby", coord_checked: "Hagendenkmal (Worms) (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Hagendenkmal_(Worms)", book_ids: ["the-nibelungenlied"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nibelungenlied", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-worms-hagen.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 532, "author": "Wikimedia-User Jivee Blau", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AWorms-_Am_Rhein-_Hagendenkmal_16.6.2013.jpg", "alt_en": "Hagen throwing the Nibelung hoard into the Rhine at Worms", "alt_zh": "沃尔姆斯莱茵河边哈根抛宝的铜像"} },
  { id: "de-hoelderlinturm", country: "de", name: "Hölderlin Tower, Tübingen", name_zh: "图宾根 · 荷尔德林塔", lat: 48.519344, lon: 9.055728, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "荷尔德林写完书信体小说《许佩里翁》后精神失常，1807 年起被一位木匠收留在内卡河边这座塔楼里，在这里度过了后半生的三十六年，直到 1843 年去世，窗外就是河。塔楼现在是他的纪念馆，从对岸的梧桐树岛上看过去最好，夏天河上撑着长篙的平底船", note_en: "After writing his novel in letters, Hyperion, Hölderlin lost his mind, and from 1807 a carpenter took him into this tower on the Neckar, where he lived for the last thirty-six years of his life, until 1843, with the river under his window. The tower is now a museum to him; the best view is from the plane-tree island opposite, and in summer punts are poled along the river below", coord_checked: "Hölderlinturm (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Hölderlinturm", book_ids: ["hyperion"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedrich_H%C3%B6lderlin", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-hoelderlinturm.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "Hedwig Storch", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AT%C3%BCbingen%2C_Neckar%2C_H%C3%B6lderlinturm_IMG_5192.JPG", "alt_en": "The Hölderlin Tower on the Neckar, Tübingen", "alt_zh": "图宾根内卡河边的荷尔德林塔"} },
  { id: "de-kleist-grave", country: "de", name: "Kleist's grave, Wannsee", name_zh: "柏林万湖 · 克莱斯特墓", lat: 52.418809, lon: 13.171914, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "1811 年 11 月 21 日，克莱斯特和身患绝症的女友亨丽埃特·福格尔在小万湖边双双开枪自尽，就葬在出事的地方。他生前不得志，死后才被奉为德语最好的小说家之一：《O 侯爵夫人》开头是一则惊人的启事，《米夏埃尔·科尔哈斯》写马贩子为讨公道烧城造反。万湖是哈弗尔河的湖湾", note_en: "On 21 November 1811 Kleist and his terminally ill friend Henriette Vogel shot themselves on the shore of the Kleiner Wannsee, and they are buried where it happened. Little recognized in his lifetime, he is now counted among the finest storytellers in German: The Marquise of O opens with an astonishing newspaper notice, and Michael Kohlhaas follows a horse dealer who burns towns in his fight for justice. The Wannsee is a bay of the Havel", coord_checked: "Kleist-Vogel-Grab (Berlin) (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Kleist-Vogel-Grab_(Berlin)", book_ids: ["the-marquise-of-o", "michael-kohlhaas"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heinrich_von_Kleist", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-kleist-grave.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 536, "author": "Jochen Teufel", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AKleistgrab_%C3%9Cberblick_%282009%29.jpg", "alt_en": "Kleist's grave by the Kleiner Wannsee", "alt_zh": "小万湖边的克莱斯特墓"} },
  { id: "de-buechnerhaus", country: "de", name: "Büchner's birthplace, Goddelau", name_zh: "戈德劳 · 毕希纳故居", lat: 49.8355, lon: 8.50012, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "毕希纳 1813 年生在这座木筋房子里，只活了二十三岁，留下的作品却改变了德语文学：《伦茨》写一位诗人在山里一步步陷入疯狂，没写完的《沃伊采克》写一个穷士兵被人折磨到杀死爱人，是第一部以底层人为主角的悲剧。故居现在是毕希纳纪念馆", note_en: "Büchner was born in this half-timbered house in 1813 and lived only twenty-three years, but what he left changed German writing: Lenz follows a poet sliding into madness in the mountains, and the unfinished Woyzeck, about a poor soldier tormented until he kills the woman he loves, is the first tragedy with a man from the bottom of society as its hero. The house is now a Büchner museum", coord_checked: "Büchnerhaus (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Büchnerhaus", book_ids: ["lenz", "woyzeck"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georg_B%C3%BCchner", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-buechnerhaus.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "No machine-readable author provided. ThomasPusch assumed (based on copyright claims).", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ARiedstadt-Goddelau_B%C3%BCchnerhaus_1.jpg", "alt_en": "Büchner's birthplace in Goddelau", "alt_zh": "戈德劳的毕希纳故居"} },
  { id: "de-leipzig-markt", country: "de", name: "Market square, Leipzig", name_zh: "莱比锡 · 集市广场", lat: 51.3404, lon: 12.3747, place_type: "prototype", prototype_of: "1824 年 8 月 27 日，退伍士兵约翰·克里斯蒂安·沃伊采克因为嫉妒刺死情人，在莱比锡老市政厅前的集市广场上被公开斩首，围观的人成千上万。法庭为他是否精神失常争论了好几年，毕希纳读了医生的鉴定报告，写成剧本《沃伊采克》。广场今天是莱比锡老城的中心", prototype_of_en: "On 27 August 1824 Johann Christian Woyzeck, a former soldier who had stabbed his lover in a fit of jealousy, was publicly beheaded on this market square in front of the Old Town Hall, before a crowd of thousands. The courts had argued for years over whether he was insane, and Büchner read the doctor's report and made it into his play Woyzeck. The square is still the heart of Leipzig's old town", coord_checked: "Markt (Leipzig) (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Markt_(Leipzig)", book_ids: ["woyzeck"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woyzeck", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-leipzig-markt.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 570, "author": "Falk2", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A01_649_Leipzig%2C_Markt.jpg", "alt_en": "The market square and Old Town Hall, Leipzig", "alt_zh": "莱比锡集市广场和老市政厅"} },
  { id: "de-osthofen", country: "de", name: "Osthofen concentration camp memorial", name_zh: "奥斯特霍芬集中营纪念馆", lat: 49.707777777778, lon: 8.3258333333333, place_type: "prototype", prototype_of: "纳粹上台后，莱茵黑森的奥斯特霍芬在一座旧造纸厂里设了最早的集中营之一，关押政敌。流亡中的安娜·西格斯把它写成小说里的韦斯特霍芬集中营：七个囚犯越狱，营长砍了七棵梧桐树做成十字架，等着把抓回来的人钉上去。旧厂房现在是纪念馆和纳粹史档案中心", prototype_of_en: "Soon after the Nazis came to power, one of the first concentration camps for their opponents was set up in an old paper factory at Osthofen in Rhenish Hesse. Anna Seghers, in exile, made it the Westhofen camp of her novel, where seven prisoners escape and the commandant has seven plane trees cut into crosses to hang them on once they're caught. The old factory is now a memorial and documentation center on the Nazi era", coord_checked: "Gedenkstätte Osthofen (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Gedenkstätte_Osthofen", book_ids: ["the-seventh-cross"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Seventh_Cross", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-osthofen.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 452, "author": "Maxlenger", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AGedenkst%C3%A4tte_Osthofen_%28ehemaliges_Konzentrationslager%29%2C_Au%C3%9Fenansicht.jpg", "alt_en": "The Osthofen memorial in the old paper factory", "alt_zh": "旧造纸厂里的奥斯特霍芬纪念馆"} },
  { id: "de-grass-house", country: "de", name: "Günter Grass House, Lübeck", name_zh: "吕贝克 · 君特·格拉斯之家", lat: 53.86932169, lon: 10.69060578, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "格拉斯 1959 年的《铁皮鼓》让战后德国文学重新被世界看见：三岁时决定不再长大的奥斯卡，敲着铁皮鼓看完了但泽和纳粹的年代。1999 年他获诺贝尔奖，晚年住在吕贝克附近。老城里这座房子是格拉斯之家，展出他的手稿，也展出他的版画和雕塑，后院有雕塑花园", note_en: "Grass's The Tin Drum (1959) put postwar German writing back in front of the world: Oskar, who decides at three never to grow any bigger, drums his way through Danzig and the Nazi years. Grass won the Nobel Prize in 1999 and spent his last years near Lübeck. This house in the old town is the Günter Grass House, showing his manuscripts as well as his prints and sculptures, with a sculpture garden behind", coord_checked: "Günter Grass-Haus (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Günter_Grass-Haus", book_ids: ["the-tin-drum"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%BCnter_Grass", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-grass-house.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 532, "author": "Thorsten Wulff", "license": "CC BY 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AGGH_Skulpturengarten_2016.jpg", "alt_en": "The sculpture garden of the Günter Grass House, Lübeck", "alt_zh": "吕贝克格拉斯之家的雕塑花园"} },
  { id: "de-krabat-mill", country: "de", name: "Krabat Mill, Schwarzkollm", name_zh: "施瓦茨科尔姆 · 克拉巴特磨坊", lat: 51.428189, lon: 14.14882, place_type: "original", note: "克拉巴特是劳齐茨地区索布人的传说人物，普鲁士勒把他写成《鬼磨坊》：少年乞丐一连三夜梦见有声音叫他去施瓦茨科尔姆的磨坊，结果成了黑魔法师的学徒。他也写了《小幽灵》等童书。村边这座磨坊是按传说重建的，夏天有露天戏剧，磨坊和小博物馆都能参观", note_en: "Krabat is a figure of Sorbian legend in Lusatia, and Preußler turned him into The Satanic Mill: a beggar boy dreams three nights running that a voice is calling him to the mill at Schwarzkollm, and becomes the apprentice of a black magician. Preußler also wrote The Little Ghost and other children's books. This mill at the edge of the village was rebuilt after the legend, with open-air theater in summer, and the mill and a small museum can be visited", coord_checked: "Krabatmühle, Schwarzkollm (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Krabatmühle,_Schwarzkollm", book_ids: ["krabat", "the-little-ghost"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krabat_(novel)", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-krabat-mill.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Oberlausitzerin64", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AHoyerswerda_Schwarzkollm_Krabatmuehle.jpg", "alt_en": "The Krabat Mill at Schwarzkollm", "alt_zh": "施瓦茨科尔姆的克拉巴特磨坊"} },
  { id: "de-ende-kurpark", country: "de", name: "Michael Ende Park, Garmisch-Partenkirchen", name_zh: "加尔米施-帕滕基兴 · 米切尔·恩德公园", lat: 47.49518, lon: 11.09111, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "米切尔·恩德 1929 年生在阿尔卑斯山脚下的加尔米施。他的《毛毛》写一个住在圆形剧场废墟里、最会倾听的小女孩，和偷走人们时间的灰先生作战；《吉姆·纽扣》则是火车头卢卡斯和黑人男孩吉姆的冒险。这座疗养公园以他命名，有露天剧场、草坪迷宫和他书里的巨龟莫拉", note_en: "Michael Ende was born in 1929 in Garmisch at the foot of the Alps. Momo is about a little girl who lives in the ruins of an amphitheater, listens better than anyone, and takes on the grey men who steal people's time; Jim Button follows the engine driver Luke and the boy Jim on their adventures. This spa park is named after him, with an open-air stage, a lawn maze and the ancient turtle Morla from his books", coord_checked: "File:Garmisch-Partenkirchen, Michael-Ende-Park, Freilichtbühne.JPG (Wikimedia Commons, geotagged) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Garmisch-Partenkirchen,_Michael-Ende-Park,_Freilichtb%C3%BChne.JPG", book_ids: ["momo", "jim-button"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Ende", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-ende-kurpark.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 524, "author": "Dguendel", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AGarmisch-Partenkirchen%2C_Michael-Ende-Park%2C_Freilichtb%C3%BChne.JPG", "alt_en": "The open-air stage in the Michael Ende Park", "alt_zh": "米切尔·恩德公园里的露天舞台"} },
  { id: "de-moelln-eulenspiegel", country: "de", name: "Eulenspiegel Museum, Mölln", name_zh: "默尔恩 · 蒂尔·欧伊伦施皮格尔博物馆", lat: 53.6308, lon: 10.6923, place_type: "prototype", prototype_of: "蒂尔·欧伊伦施皮格尔是中世纪德国传说里专门捉弄人的小丑，传说他 1350 年死在默尔恩，教堂外墙上还有他的墓碑。克尔曼的《蒂尔》把他挪到了三十年战争，让他在烧杀和瘟疫里一路杂耍。市集广场上有他的铜像，旁边这座老房子是欧伊伦施皮格尔博物馆", prototype_of_en: "Till Eulenspiegel is the trickster jester of medieval German legend, said to have died in Mölln in 1350, and his gravestone is still on the church wall. Kehlmann's Tyll moves him into the Thirty Years' War and has him juggle his way through its burning and plague. His bronze stands on the market square, and this old house beside it is the Eulenspiegel Museum", coord_checked: "Am Markt 2 (Mölln) (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Am_Markt_2_(Mölln)", book_ids: ["tyll"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyll_(novel)", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-moelln-eulenspiegel.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Klaus Graf", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ASchleswig_holstein_2011_036.jpg", "alt_en": "Till Eulenspiegel in the Eulenspiegel Museum, Mölln", "alt_zh": "默尔恩欧伊伦施皮格尔博物馆里的蒂尔像"} },
  { id: "de-goettingen-observatory", country: "de", name: "Göttingen Observatory", name_zh: "哥廷根大学天文台", lat: 51.5285, lon: 9.94308, place_type: "original", note: "数学家高斯 1807 年起当哥廷根天文台台长，在这里住到 1855 年去世，几乎一辈子没离开过这座城。克尔曼的《丈量世界》把他和到处探险的洪堡写成一对：一个坐在家里算出世界，一个跑遍美洲去量它。古典主义风格的天文台楼保存完好，现在是大学的研究院", note_en: "The mathematician Gauss became director of the Göttingen Observatory in 1807 and lived here until his death in 1855, hardly ever leaving the town. Kehlmann's Measuring the World pairs him with the explorer Humboldt: one stays at home and works out the world, the other travels the Americas to measure it. The neoclassical observatory building survives intact and now houses a university institute", coord_checked: "Universitäts-Sternwarte Göttingen (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Universitäts-Sternwarte_Göttingen", book_ids: ["measuring-the-world"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Measuring_the_World", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-goettingen-observatory.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "User Patrickhessman on de.wikipedia", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ASternwarte_goettingen.jpg", "alt_en": "The Göttingen Observatory, where Gauss lived", "alt_zh": "高斯住过的哥廷根天文台"} },
  { id: "de-weisser-hirsch", country: "de", name: "Weißer Hirsch, Dresden", name_zh: "德累斯顿 · 白鹿区", lat: 51.063888888889, lon: 13.821388888889, place_type: "original", note: "易北河北岸山坡上的白鹿区满是十九世纪的别墅和疗养院。特尔坎普在这里长大，《塔》写的就是民主德国最后七年里住在这片别墅区的医生、出版人和学者，他们守着旧家具和藏书，像活在另一个世纪，直到 1989 年一切崩塌。今天可以坐缆车从易北河边上来", note_en: "The Weißer Hirsch quarter on the slopes above the Elbe is full of nineteenth-century villas and old sanatoriums. Tellkamp grew up here, and The Tower follows the doctors, publishers and scholars living in these villas through the last seven years of East Germany, holding on to their old furniture and books as if in another century until everything collapses in 1989. A funicular brings you up from the Elbe", coord_checked: "Weißer Hirsch (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Weißer_Hirsch", book_ids: ["the-tower"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Tower_(Tellkamp_novel)", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-weisser-hirsch.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 611, "author": ".mw-parser-output .commons-creator-table{background-color:#f0f0ff;box-sizing:border-box;font-size:95%;text-align:start;c", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A20060505080DR_Dresden-Wei%C3%9Fer_Hirsch_Strau%C3%9Fstra%C3%9Fe_1.jpg", "alt_en": "A villa in the Weißer Hirsch quarter, Dresden", "alt_zh": "德累斯顿白鹿区的一座别墅"} },
  { id: "de-marzahn", country: "de", name: "Marzahn, Berlin", name_zh: "柏林 · 马灿", lat: 52.5458, lon: 13.5625, place_type: "original", note: "马灿是东柏林边上民主德国时代建起的大片预制板住宅区。赫恩多夫的《契克》就从这里出发：十四岁的迈克家住马灿的一座别墅，暑假被父母丢下，俄罗斯转学生契克开来一辆偷来的拉达，两人不带地图就开进了勃兰登堡乡下。登上高楼的观景台能看到一整片板楼", note_en: "Marzahn, on the eastern edge of Berlin, is a vast estate of prefabricated housing built in East German times. Herrndorf's Why We Took the Car sets out from here: fourteen-year-old Maik, whose family has a villa in Marzahn, is left alone for the summer, and Tschick, the new boy from Russia, turns up in a stolen Lada, and the two of them drive off into the Brandenburg countryside with no map. From the skywalk on top of a tower block you can see the whole sea of slab buildings", coord_checked: "Berlin-Marzahn (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Berlin-Marzahn", book_ids: ["why-we-took-the-car"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Why_We_Took_the_Car", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-marzahn.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 528, "author": "Singlespeedfahrer", "license": "CC0", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AAussicht_Skywalk_Marzahn_Plattenbau_Fassendekoration_Jan-Petersen-Stra%C3%9Fe_Berlin-Marzahn_02.jpg", "alt_en": "Prefabricated tower blocks in Marzahn", "alt_zh": "马灿的预制板高楼"} },
  { id: "de-emmertsgrund", country: "de", name: "Emmertsgrund, Heidelberg", name_zh: "海德堡 · 埃默茨格伦德", lat: 49.3669, lon: 8.70417, place_type: "original", note: "1992 年，十四岁的斯坦尼西奇随家人逃离波黑战争来到海德堡，住进山坡上的高楼区埃默茨格伦德。《出身》里写，这里最出力帮外来孩子融入的，是一座破旧的阿拉尔加油站，大家天天在那儿混。这片住宅区离海德堡老城不远，站在坡上能望见莱茵平原", note_en: "In 1992, at fourteen, Stanišić fled the war in Bosnia with his family and came to Heidelberg, to the high-rise estate of Emmertsgrund on the hillside. In Where You Come From, the institution that does most to help the newcomers fit in is a run-down Aral petrol station where everyone hangs out. The estate is not far from the old town, and from the slope you look out over the Rhine plain", coord_checked: "Emmertsgrund (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Emmertsgrund", book_ids: ["where-you-come-from"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sa%C5%A1a_Stani%C5%A1i%C4%87", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-emmertsgrund.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 534, "author": "R. J. Mathar", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AEmmertsgrund_2.jpg", "alt_en": "The high-rises of Emmertsgrund on the hillside above Heidelberg", "alt_zh": "海德堡山坡上的埃默茨格伦德高楼"} },
  { id: "de-ingolstadt-anatomy", country: "de", name: "Old Anatomy, Ingolstadt", name_zh: "因戈尔施塔特 · 老解剖楼", lat: 48.762536111111, lon: 11.419327777778, place_type: "original", note: "《弗兰肯斯坦》里，日内瓦青年维克多来到因戈尔施塔特上大学，迷上了生命的秘密，在这里拼出了那个怪物。这所大学当时是巴伐利亚最古老的大学，医学和解剖很有名。大学的老解剖楼建于 18 世纪，现在是德国医学史博物馆，后面还有一座药草园", note_en: "In Frankenstein, Victor comes from Geneva to study at the university of Ingolstadt, becomes obsessed with the secret of life, and puts his creature together here. It was then the oldest university in Bavaria, with a well-known medical faculty. The university's eighteenth-century anatomy building is now the German Museum of Medical History, with a medicinal herb garden behind it", coord_checked: "Alte Anatomie (Ingolstadt) (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Alte_Anatomie_(Ingolstadt)", book_ids: ["frankenstein"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frankenstein", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-ingolstadt-anatomy.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 532, "author": "Chrisi1964", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlte_Anatomie_in_Ingolstadt.jpg", "alt_en": "The Old Anatomy building, Ingolstadt", "alt_zh": "因戈尔施塔特的老解剖楼"} },
  { id: "de-dachau", country: "de", name: "Dachau concentration camp memorial", name_zh: "达豪集中营纪念馆", lat: 48.2703, lon: 11.4681, place_type: "original", note: "《偷书贼》的小镇莫尔兴在慕尼黑附近，是虚构的，书里的达豪集中营却是真的：犹太囚犯被押着穿过小镇去达豪，养父汉斯给一个老人递了块面包，两人都挨了鞭子。藏在地下室的马克斯后来也被送进这里。集中营是纳粹最早建的一座，如今是纪念馆，免费参观", note_en: "Molching, the little town near Munich in The Book Thief, is invented, but the Dachau camp in the book is real: Jewish prisoners are marched through the town on their way there, and when Liesel's foster father Hans hands an old man a piece of bread, both are whipped. Max, hidden in the basement, is later sent here too. One of the first camps the Nazis built, it is now a memorial, free to visit", coord_checked: "Dachau concentration camp memorial site (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Dachau_concentration_camp_memorial_site", book_ids: ["the-book-thief"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Book_Thief", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-dachau.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "Eivind Saiwalo", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A160929_Dachau_KZ.jpg", "alt_en": "The roll-call square of the Dachau memorial", "alt_zh": "达豪纪念馆的点名广场"} },
  { id: "de-checkpoint-charlie", country: "de", name: "Checkpoint Charlie, Berlin", name_zh: "柏林 · 查理检查站", lat: 52.5075, lon: 13.390277777778, place_type: "original", note: "冷战时，弗里德里希街上的查理检查站是东西柏林之间最有名的过境点。勒卡雷的《柏林谍影》开头，英国驻柏林情报站长利玛斯在检查站熬了一夜，等最后一名特工从东边过来，却眼看他在边境线前被枪杀。今天路中间还立着复制的岗亭，旁边有柏林墙博物馆", note_en: "During the Cold War, Checkpoint Charlie on the Friedrichstraße was the best-known crossing between East and West Berlin. The Spy Who Came in from the Cold opens with Leamas, the head of British intelligence in Berlin, waiting all night at the checkpoint for his last agent to come over from the East, only to watch him shot down short of the line. A replica guard hut stands in the middle of the street, and the Wall Museum is beside it", coord_checked: "Checkpoint Charlie (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Checkpoint_Charlie", book_ids: ["the-spy-who-came-in-from-the-cold"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Spy_Who_Came_in_from_the_Cold", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-checkpoint-charlie.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "Paul Korecky", "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A2018-08-09_DE_Berlin-Friedrichshain-Kreuzberg%2C_Friedrichstra%C3%9Fe%2C_Checkpoint_Charlie_%2849918288678%29.jpg", "alt_en": "Checkpoint Charlie on the Friedrichstraße", "alt_zh": "弗里德里希街上的查理检查站"} },
  { id: "de-schillerhaus-weimar", country: "de", name: "Schiller's House, Weimar", name_zh: "魏玛 · 席勒故居", lat: 50.9789, lon: 11.3278, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "席勒年轻时写《强盗》《阴谋与爱情》，一个贵族青年爱上乐师的女儿，被宫廷阴谋逼死，台下观众都哭了。1799 年他搬到魏玛，和歌德成了挚友，第二年写成《玛利亚·斯图亚特》。1802 年起他住在这座黄房子里，1805 年在这里去世，阁楼书房和他的书桌都还在", note_en: "As a young man Schiller wrote The Robbers and Intrigue and Love, in which a young nobleman's love for a musician's daughter is destroyed by court intrigue, and audiences wept. In 1799 he moved to Weimar, became Goethe's close friend, and finished Mary Stuart the next year. From 1802 he lived in this yellow house, where he died in 1805; his attic study and desk are still there", coord_checked: "Schillerhaus Weimar (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Schillerhaus_Weimar", book_ids: ["mary-stuart", "intrigue-and-love"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedrich_Schiller", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-schillerhaus-weimar.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 528, "author": "Andreas Trepte", "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ASchiller_Weimar.jpg", "alt_en": "Schiller's house in Weimar", "alt_zh": "魏玛的席勒故居"} },
  { id: "de-lessinghaus", country: "de", name: "Lessing House, Wolfenbüttel", name_zh: "沃尔芬比特尔 · 莱辛故居", lat: 52.16367, lon: 10.53012, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "莱辛 1770 年起在沃尔芬比特尔当公爵图书馆馆长，晚年住在图书馆旁这座房子里，1779 年写成《智者纳旦》：一个犹太商人用三枚戒指的寓言回答苏丹，劝三大宗教彼此宽容。他早年的《明娜·冯·巴尔赫姆》是德语最早的喜剧杰作。故居现在是纪念馆，隔壁就是奥古斯特公爵图书馆", note_en: "Lessing became librarian of the ducal library at Wolfenbüttel in 1770 and in his last years lived in this house beside it, where in 1779 he wrote Nathan the Wise: a Jewish merchant answers the Sultan with the parable of the three rings and pleads for tolerance between the three faiths. His earlier Minna von Barnhelm was the first great comedy in German. The house is a museum, next door to the Herzog August Library", coord_checked: "File:Lessinghaus Wolfenbüttel 2014.jpg (Wikimedia Commons, geotagged) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lessinghaus_Wolfenb%C3%BCttel_2014.jpg", book_ids: ["nathan-the-wise", "minna-von-barnhelm"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gotthold_Ephraim_Lessing", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-lessinghaus.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "Stefan Schäfer, Lich", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ALessinghaus_Wolfenb%C3%BCttel_2014.jpg", "alt_en": "The Lessing House in Wolfenbüttel", "alt_zh": "沃尔芬比特尔的莱辛故居"} },
  { id: "de-storm-husum", country: "de", name: "Theodor Storm House, Husum", name_zh: "胡苏姆 · 施托姆故居", lat: 54.4757, lon: 9.04766, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "施托姆 1817 年生在北海边的小城胡苏姆，称它是“海边的灰色小城”。他最后的中篇《白马骑士》写北弗里斯兰的一个堤长骑着白马修新堤，与洪水和村民的迷信对抗，传说风暴夜里还能看见他的白马在堤上奔跑。他住过的这座房子现在是施托姆博物馆", note_en: "Storm was born in 1817 in Husum on the North Sea, which he called 'the grey town by the sea'. His last novella, The Rider on the White Horse, tells of a North Frisian dikemaster who builds a new dike on his white horse against floods and the villagers' superstitions; on stormy nights, they say, his horse can still be seen galloping on the dike. The house where he lived is now the Storm museum", coord_checked: "Theodor-Storm-Haus (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Theodor-Storm-Haus", book_ids: ["the-rider-on-the-white-horse"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Rider_on_the_White_Horse", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-storm-husum.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": ".mw-parser-output .commons-creator-table{background-color:#f0f0ff;box-sizing:border-box;font-size:95%;text-align:start;c", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AHusum-Theodor-Storm-Haus-msu-2020-9-8443.jpg", "alt_en": "The Theodor Storm House in Husum", "alt_zh": "胡苏姆的施托姆故居"} },
  { id: "de-remarque-osnabrueck", country: "de", name: "Remarque Peace Centre, Osnabrück", name_zh: "奥斯纳布吕克 · 雷马克和平中心", lat: 52.277344444444, lon: 8.0419833333333, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "雷马克 1898 年生在奥斯纳布吕克，十八岁应征入伍，上了西线。1929 年的《西线无战事》用一个年轻士兵的口吻写战壕里的死亡，被纳粹焚毁，却成了全世界读得最多的反战小说。他的《黑色方尖碑》把家乡写成小城维尔登布吕克。市集广场边的这座楼是雷马克和平中心", note_en: "Remarque was born in Osnabrück in 1898, was called up at eighteen and sent to the Western Front. All Quiet on the Western Front (1929) tells of death in the trenches in the voice of a young soldier; the Nazis burned it, and it became the most widely read antiwar novel in the world. In The Black Obelisk he turned his home town into the little town of Werdenbrück. This building on the market square is the Remarque Peace Centre", coord_checked: "Erich Maria Remarque-Friedenszentrum (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Erich_Maria_Remarque-Friedenszentrum", book_ids: ["all-quiet-on-the-western-front", "the-black-obelisk"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erich_Maria_Remarque", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-remarque-osnabrueck.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "Ramsch", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AErich_Maria_Remarque-Friedenszentrum_am_Markt.jpg", "alt_en": "The Remarque Peace Centre on Osnabrück's market square", "alt_zh": "奥斯纳布吕克集市广场边的雷马克和平中心"} },
  { id: "de-berliner-ensemble", country: "de", name: "Berliner Ensemble", name_zh: "柏林剧团", lat: 52.521666944444, lon: 13.386111111111, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "1928 年布莱希特的《三分钱歌剧》在施普雷河边这座剧院首演，一炮而红。流亡归来后，他和妻子海伦妮·魏格尔 1949 年创办柏林剧团，魏格尔演的《大胆妈妈》推着货车在三十年战争里做买卖、一个个失去孩子，成了剧团的招牌。1954 年剧团搬进这里，门前广场上有布莱希特的坐像", note_en: "Brecht's Threepenny Opera opened in this theater by the Spree in 1928 and was an instant hit. Back from exile, he and his wife Helene Weigel founded the Berliner Ensemble in 1949, and Weigel's Mother Courage, hauling her cart through the Thirty Years' War and losing her children one by one, became the company's signature. The company moved in here in 1954, and Brecht sits in bronze on the square in front", coord_checked: "Berliner Ensemble (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Berliner_Ensemble", book_ids: ["mother-courage", "life-of-galileo"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berliner_Ensemble", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-berliner-ensemble.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 580, "author": "Yair Haklai", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ABerliner_Ensemble_building_%28Theater_am_Schiffbauerdamm%29.jpg", "alt_en": "The Berliner Ensemble's theater on the Schiffbauerdamm", "alt_zh": "船坞剧院里的柏林剧团"} },
  { id: "de-bonn-hbf", country: "de", name: "Bonn Hauptbahnhof", name_zh: "波恩火车总站", lat: 50.731944444444, lon: 7.0969444444444, place_type: "original", note: "伯尔的《小丑之见》里，丑角汉斯·施尼尔丢了工作也丢了爱人，回到当时西德首都波恩的公寓，整夜打电话求人。小说最后，他抹上白脸、抱着吉他，坐在波恩火车站的台阶上卖唱，等玛丽从罗马回来。这座红砖站房是 19 世纪末的老建筑，至今还在用", note_en: "In Böll's The Clown, the clown Hans Schnier, out of work and abandoned by the woman he loves, comes back to his flat in Bonn, then the West German capital, and spends the night on the phone begging for help. At the end he paints his face white, takes his guitar and sits on the steps of Bonn station singing for coins, waiting for Marie to come back from Rome. The red station building dates from the late nineteenth century and is still in use", coord_checked: "Bonn Hauptbahnhof (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Bonn_Hauptbahnhof", book_ids: ["the-clown"], fact_source: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Clown_(novel)", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-bonn-hbf.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "➨ Redvers from Belgium/UK", "license": "CC BY 2.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ABonn_Hauptbahnhof.jpg", "alt_en": "Bonn's main station", "alt_zh": "波恩火车总站"} },
  { id: "de-heidelberg-old-university", country: "de", name: "Old University, Heidelberg", name_zh: "海德堡 · 老大学", lat: 49.4111, lon: 8.70611, place_type: "original", note: "《朗读者》里十五岁的米夏埃尔爱上了比他大二十岁的汉娜，几年后在法学院的研讨课上，竟在纳粹集中营看守的审判席上认出了她。书里没写城名，海德堡市官方说故事就发生在这里，施林克本人也在海德堡读法律。大学广场上的老大学楼里有大学博物馆和学生禁闭室", note_en: "In The Reader, fifteen-year-old Michael falls in love with Hanna, twenty years older; years later, at a law-school seminar, he recognizes her in the dock at the trial of concentration-camp guards. The book never names its city, but Heidelberg says the story is set here, and Schlink himself studied law in Heidelberg. The Old University on the university square has the university museum and the old student prison", coord_checked: "Alte Universität (Heidelberg) (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Alte_Universität_(Heidelberg)", book_ids: ["the-reader"], fact_source: "https://www.heidelberg.de/HD/Leben/Autoren.html", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-heidelberg-old-university.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Ribax ( Diskussion ) 22:55, 7. Mär. 2014 (CET)", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ADer_Universit%C3%A4tsplatz_in_Heidelberg_an_einem_Sp%C3%A4therbst_Nachmittag.JPG", "alt_en": "The university square in Heidelberg", "alt_zh": "海德堡大学广场"} },
  { id: "de-potsdam-academy", country: "de", name: "Former Academy of Political and Legal Sciences, Potsdam", name_zh: "波茨坦 · 原国家与法学研究院", lat: 52.3928, lon: 13.1273, place_type: "original", note: "《光芒渐逝的年代》写一个东德家庭四代人。祖母夏洛特和继祖父威廉从墨西哥流亡归来，住在波茨坦附近诺伊恩多夫的别墅里，夏洛特就在当地的国家与法学研究院工作。这座大楼当年正是民主德国的国家与法学研究院，现在是波茨坦大学格里布尼茨湖校区的主楼", note_en: "In Times of Fading Light follows four generations of an East German family. The grandmother Charlotte and her husband Wilhelm come back from exile in Mexico to a villa in Neuendorf near Potsdam, and Charlotte works at the Academy of Political and Legal Sciences there. This building really was East Germany's Academy of Political and Legal Sciences, and is now the main building of the University of Potsdam's Griebnitzsee campus", coord_checked: "Ehemaliges Präsidialgebäude des Deutschen Roten Kreuzes (Wikimedia Commons) — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Ehemaliges_Präsidialgebäude_des_Deutschen_Roten_Kreuzes", book_ids: ["in-times-of-fading-light"], fact_source: "https://dewiki.de/Lexikon/In_Zeiten_des_abnehmenden_Lichts", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-potsdam-academy.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Jorges at German Wikipedia", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AUniversitaet_Potsdam_-_Babelsberg_-_Haus_1.jpg", "alt_en": "The former academy building, now the University of Potsdam at Griebnitzsee", "alt_zh": "原国家与法学研究院大楼，今波茨坦大学格里布尼茨湖校区"} },
  // —— 11-23: second pack (tools/content/batches/add_de1.py) ——
  { id: "de-kickelhahn", country: "de", name: "Kickelhahn (Goethe's hut), Ilmenau", name_zh: "基克尔汉山（歌德小屋），伊尔默瑙", lat: 50.665556, lon: 10.881389, place_type: "original", note: "1780 年 9 月 6 日傍晚，歌德在伊尔默瑙上方基克尔汉山顶附近的一间猎人木屋的板壁上写下了这首八行小诗。1831 年 8 月，他在最后一个生日的前一天重回这里，又读了一遍。木屋 1870 年烧毁，后来重建", note_en: "On the evening of 6 September 1780 Goethe wrote the eight lines of this poem on the wooden wall of a hunters' hut near the top of the Kickelhahn, above Ilmenau. He came back in August 1831, the day before his last birthday, and read them again. The hut burned down in 1870 and was rebuilt", coord_checked: "Kickelhahn (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kickelhahn, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-kickelhahn.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "Dguendel", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AIlmenau%2C_Kickelhahn%2C_am_Goetheh%C3%A4uschen.jpg", "alt_en": "On the Kickelhahn beside the Goethe hut, looking out over the Thuringian Forest", "alt_zh": "基克尔汉山上歌德小屋旁，眺望图林根森林"} },
  { id: "de-marbach", country: "de", name: "Marbach am Neckar (German Literature Archive)", name_zh: "内卡河畔马尔巴赫（德国文学档案馆）", lat: 48.93602, lon: 9.25665, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "席勒 1759 年生于马尔巴赫，出生的房子今为纪念馆。城外山坡上是席勒国家博物馆和德国文学档案馆，后者收藏着十八世纪至今德语作家的手稿和遗物", book_ids: ["the-robbers", "intrigue-and-love", "mary-stuart"], note_en: "Schiller was born in Marbach in 1759; the house of his birth is a museum. On the hill above the town stand the Schiller National Museum and the German Literature Archive, which keeps the papers of German writers from the eighteenth century to the present", coord_checked: "Deutsches Literaturarchiv Marbach (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deutsches_Literaturarchiv_Marbach, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-marbach.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 723, "author": "Bildtechnik Braendle", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AFriedrich_Schillers_Geburtshaus_in_Marbach_am_Neckar.jpg", "alt_en": "Schiller's birthplace in Marbach", "alt_zh": "马尔巴赫的席勒出生地"} },
  { id: "de-heidelberg-old-bridge", country: "de", name: "Old Bridge, Heidelberg", name_zh: "老桥，海德堡", lat: 49.414253, lon: 8.709547, place_type: "original", note: "内卡河上的石桥，1788 年建成。荷尔德林约 1800 年写下的这首献给海德堡的颂歌，回忆的就是他年轻时路过此地、站在桥上着了迷的那一刻", note_en: "The stone bridge over the Neckar, finished in 1788. Hölderlin's ode to the town, written around 1800, remembers standing on it spellbound as a young man passing through", coord_checked: "Alte Brücke (Heidelberg) (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alte_Br%C3%BCcke_(Heidelberg), 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-heidelberg-old-bridge.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 534, "author": "Flocci Nivis", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A20230315_Hotel_Holl%C3%A4nder_Hof_Heidelberg_02.jpg", "alt_en": "The gate of the Old Bridge and the old town, seen from the bridge", "alt_zh": "从老桥上望桥头城门和老城"} },
  { id: "de-goettingen-rathaus", country: "de", name: "Old Town Hall, Göttingen", name_zh: "老市政厅，哥廷根", lat: 51.532878, lon: 9.934681, place_type: "original", note: "海涅在哥廷根学法律，因决斗事件被勒令离校，后来又回来，1825 年在这里取得博士学位。1824 年 9 月他从这座城徒步前往哈尔茨山，游记一开头就把哥廷根和它的教授们挖苦了一番", note_en: "Heine studied law at Göttingen, was sent down for a duelling affair, came back and took his doctorate here in 1825. In September 1824 he set off from the town on foot for the Harz, and his account of the walk begins with a mocking portrait of Göttingen and its professors", coord_checked: "Altes Rathaus (Göttingen) (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Altes_Rathaus_(G%C3%B6ttingen), 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-goettingen-rathaus.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "Soenke Rahn", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AAltes_Rathaus_mit_G%C3%A4nseliesel_%28G%C3%B6ttingen%29.JPG", "alt_en": "The Old Town Hall and the Gänseliesel fountain in Göttingen", "alt_zh": "哥廷根老市政厅和牧鹅少女喷泉"} },
  { id: "de-cologne-cathedral", country: "de", name: "Cologne Cathedral", name_zh: "科隆大教堂", lat: 50.94129, lon: 6.95817, place_type: "original", note: "1843 年海涅路过时，大教堂还只是半截子工程：十六世纪停工，1842 年才刚刚复工。他嘲笑了那个募款要把它建完的爱国协会。大教堂 1880 年落成", note_en: "When Heine passed through in 1843 the cathedral was still a torso: building had stopped in the sixteenth century and had only just begun again in 1842. He mocked the patriotic society that was collecting to finish it. It was completed in 1880", coord_checked: "Kölner Dom (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/K%C3%B6lner_Dom, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-cologne-cathedral.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "Elisabeth Schittenhelm", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AWestfassade_des_K%C3%B6lner_Doms_mit_Richter-Fenster.jpg", "alt_en": "The west front of Cologne Cathedral", "alt_zh": "科隆大教堂的西立面"} },
  { id: "de-kyffhaeuser", country: "de", name: "Kyffhäuser", name_zh: "屈夫霍伊泽山", lat: 51.413056, lon: 11.109722, place_type: "original", note: "图林根北部林木覆盖的山岭。传说红胡子皇帝腓特烈一世和他的骑士们睡在山洞里，等到德国需要他的时候才醒来。海涅让老乳母给他讲这个传说，然后自己进山去同皇帝争辩。山上那座巨大的纪念碑建于 1890 年代", note_en: "The wooded ridge in northern Thuringia where, legend says, the emperor Frederick Barbarossa sleeps in a cave with his knights until Germany needs him. Heine has his old nurse tell him the legend, and then goes into the mountain to argue with the emperor. The huge monument on the ridge was built in the 1890s", coord_checked: "Kyffhäuserdenkmal (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kyffh%C3%A4userdenkmal, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-kyffhaeuser.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "kirq (Tomasz Halszka)", "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AKyffhaeuserdenkmal_front_view.JPG", "alt_en": "The Kyffhäuser monument with the figure of Barbarossa", "alt_zh": "屈夫霍伊泽纪念碑和红胡子皇帝像"} },
  { id: "de-grimmwelt", country: "de", name: "Grimmwelt, Kassel", name_zh: "格林世界博物馆，卡塞尔", lat: 51.3092, lon: 9.48927, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "格林兄弟在卡塞尔住了约三十年，大部分童话是在这里搜集的。这座 2015 年开馆的博物馆展出他们亲手批注的《儿童与家庭童话集》", book_ids: ["grimms-fairy-tales"], note_en: "The brothers Grimm lived in Kassel for some thirty years and gathered most of their tales here. The museum, opened in 2015, shows their own annotated copies of the Children's and Household Tales", coord_checked: "Grimmwelt Kassel (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grimmwelt_Kassel, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-grimmwelt.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "XenonX3", "license": "CC0", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AGrimmwelt_Kassel%2C_Eingang.jpg", "alt_en": "The entrance of the Grimmwelt in Kassel", "alt_zh": "卡塞尔格林世界博物馆的入口"} },
  { id: "de-hanau-grimm", country: "de", name: "Brothers Grimm Monument, Hanau", name_zh: "格林兄弟纪念像，哈瑙", lat: 50.132993, lon: 8.916988, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "雅各布·格林和威廉·格林分别于 1785 年和 1786 年生于哈瑙。集市广场上的兄弟二人铜像 1896 年揭幕，是“德国童话之路”的起点", book_ids: ["grimms-fairy-tales"], note_en: "Jacob and Wilhelm Grimm were born in Hanau, in 1785 and 1786. The bronze of the two brothers on the market square, unveiled in 1896, is the starting point of the German Fairy Tale Route", coord_checked: "Brüder-Grimm-Nationaldenkmal (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Br%C3%BCder-Grimm-Nationaldenkmal, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-hanau-grimm.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 450, "author": "Oktobersonne", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ABr%C3%BCder-Grimm-NationaldenkmalL1070860.jpg", "alt_en": "A relief on the pedestal of the Brothers Grimm monument in Hanau", "alt_zh": "哈瑙格林兄弟纪念像基座上的浮雕"} },
  { id: "de-lusamgaertchen", country: "de", name: "Walther's Grave, Lusamgärtchen, Würzburg", name_zh: "瓦尔特墓，卢萨姆小园，维尔茨堡", lat: 49.794083, lon: 9.93175, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "中世纪最伟大的恋歌诗人瓦尔特·冯·德·福格尔魏德，据说约 1230 年葬在维尔茨堡新明斯特教堂的回廊里。回廊花园残存的一角立着一块纪念石，来访的人会在上面放花", book_ids: ["under-der-linden"], note_en: "Walther von der Vogelweide, the greatest of the Minnesingers, is said to have been buried around 1230 in the cloister of the Neumünster in Würzburg. A memorial stone stands in what remains of the cloister garden, and visitors leave flowers on it", coord_checked: "Lusamgärtchen (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lusamg%C3%A4rtchen, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-lusamgaertchen.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 502, "author": "Holger Uwe Schmitt", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AHier_im_Lusamg%C3%A4rtchen_soll_Walther_von_der_Vogelweide_begraben_worden_sein._04.jpg", "alt_en": "The Lusamgärtchen with its Romanesque arcade", "alt_zh": "卢萨姆小园和罗马式拱廊"} },
  { id: "de-wartburg", country: "de", name: "Wartburg", name_zh: "瓦尔特堡", lat: 50.966111, lon: 10.306422, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "1521 年 5 月到 1522 年 3 月，路德化名“容克尔·约尔格”藏身于艾森纳赫上方的这座城堡，用大约十一个星期把《新约》译成德文，1522 年 9 月刊印。传说中世纪恋歌诗人的赛歌会也发生在这里", book_ids: ["luther-bible"], note_en: "Hidden in this castle above Eisenach from May 1521 to March 1522 under the name 'Junker Jörg', Luther translated the New Testament into German in about eleven weeks. It was printed in September 1522. Legend also sets the medieval contest of the Minnesingers here", coord_checked: "Wartburg (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wartburg, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-wartburg.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 452, "author": "Krzysztof Golik", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AOutside_view_of_Wartburg_Castle_%284%29.jpg", "alt_en": "The gatehouse of the Wartburg", "alt_zh": "瓦尔特堡的门楼"} },
  { id: "de-gelnhausen", country: "de", name: "Gelnhausen", name_zh: "盖尔恩豪森", lat: 50.200833, lon: 9.191944, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "格里美尔斯豪森约 1622 年生于金齐希河畔的这座帝国城市，1634 年城遭洗劫，他失去了家。他那部写三十年战争的小说里，少年主人公就在附近的施佩萨特山中的农庄长大", book_ids: ["simplicissimus"], note_en: "Grimmelshausen was born in this imperial town on the Kinzig around 1622 and lost his home when it was sacked in 1634. The boy hero of his novel of the Thirty Years' War grows up on a farm in the Spessart hills nearby", coord_checked: "Gelnhausen (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gelnhausen, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-gelnhausen.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Chris06", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ASchmidtgasse_12_Gelnhausen_Geburtshaus_Grimmelshausen_%282%29.jpg", "alt_en": "The plaque on Grimmelshausen's birthplace in Gelnhausen", "alt_zh": "盖尔恩豪森格里美尔斯豪森出生地的铭牌"} },
  { id: "de-jagsthausen", country: "de", name: "Jagsthausen Castle", name_zh: "雅格斯特豪森城堡", lat: 49.31215, lon: 9.46919, place_type: "original", note: "雅格斯特河畔的城堡，“铁手骑士”葛兹·冯·贝利欣根约 1480 年生于此；歌德这部剧的许多场景就设在城堡里。每年夏天，这部剧都在城堡院子里上演", note_en: "The castle on the Jagst where the knight Götz von Berlichingen, the man with the iron hand, was born around 1480; much of Goethe's play is set in it. The play is performed every summer in the castle courtyard", coord_checked: "Burg Jagsthausen (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burg_Jagsthausen, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-jagsthausen.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "p.schmelzle", "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AJagsthausen-goetzenburg-web.jpg", "alt_en": "The Götzenburg at Jagsthausen", "alt_zh": "雅格斯特豪森的葛兹城堡"} },
  { id: "de-fehrbellin", country: "de", name: "Fehrbellin", name_zh: "费尔贝林", lat: 52.814361, lon: 12.766139, place_type: "original", note: "勃兰登堡的小城。1675 年 6 月，大选帝侯腓特烈·威廉在这里打败了瑞典军队。克莱斯特的最后一部剧作就以这场战役为背景", note_en: "The small town in Brandenburg where, in June 1675, the Great Elector Frederick William defeated the Swedes. Kleist's last play is built on the battle", coord_checked: "Fehrbellin (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fehrbellin, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-fehrbellin.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Muck", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ASiegess%C3%A4ule_Hakenberg-03-Detailansicht_Sockel.jpg", "alt_en": "The base of the victory column at Hakenberg, with the Great Elector's bust", "alt_zh": "哈肯贝格胜利纪念柱的基座和大选帝侯胸像"} },
  { id: "de-kleist-museum", country: "de", name: "Kleist Museum, Frankfurt (Oder)", name_zh: "克莱斯特博物馆，奥得河畔法兰克福", lat: 52.3438, lon: 14.55728, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "海因里希·冯·克莱斯特 1777 年生于奥得河畔法兰克福。纪念他生平和作品的博物馆设在河边从前的驻军学校里", book_ids: ["michael-kohlhaas", "the-marquise-of-o", "prince-of-homburg"], note_en: "Heinrich von Kleist was born in Frankfurt on the Oder in 1777. The museum of his life and work is in the former garrison school by the river", coord_checked: "Kleist-Museum (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kleist-Museum, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-kleist-museum.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 602, "author": "Acediscovery", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AKleist-Museum-Frankfurt-an-der-Oder-February-2025.jpg", "alt_en": "The Kleist Museum in Frankfurt (Oder)", "alt_zh": "奥得河畔法兰克福的克莱斯特博物馆"} },
  { id: "de-novalis", country: "de", name: "Novalis's Birthplace, Schloss Oberwiederstedt", name_zh: "诺瓦利斯出生地，上维德施泰特宫", lat: 51.666319, lon: 11.530669, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "弗里德里希·冯·哈登贝格（笔名诺瓦利斯）1772 年生于这座庄园宅邸。他未完成的长篇小说给了浪漫派一个象征：蓝花。宅子里设有诺瓦利斯博物馆和早期浪漫派研究中心", book_ids: ["heinrich-von-ofterdingen"], note_en: "Friedrich von Hardenberg, who called himself Novalis, was born in this manor house in 1772. His unfinished novel gave Romanticism its emblem, the blue flower. The house holds a Novalis museum and a research centre for early Romanticism", coord_checked: "Schloss Oberwiederstedt (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schloss_Oberwiederstedt, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-novalis.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Benutzer:Hejkal", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0 de", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/de/deed.en", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ASchloss_Oberwiederstedt.jpg", "alt_en": "Schloss Oberwiederstedt", "alt_zh": "上维德施泰特宫"} },
  { id: "de-dresden-albertplatz", country: "de", name: "Site of the Black Gate (Albertplatz), Dresden", name_zh: "黑门旧址（阿尔伯特广场），德累斯顿", lat: 51.063306, lon: 13.746917, place_type: "original", note: "黑门是德累斯顿新城的北门，十九世纪初拆除，原址上修了阿尔伯特广场。霍夫曼 1813 年到 1814 年住在德累斯顿，他这篇童话一开头，就是一个大学生从黑门跑过", note_en: "The Black Gate was the northern gate of Dresden's Neustadt; it was pulled down in the early nineteenth century, and Albertplatz was laid out where it stood. Hoffmann, who lived in Dresden in 1813 and 1814, starts his fairy tale with a student running through it", coord_checked: "Albertplatz (Dresden) (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albertplatz_(Dresden), 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-dresden-albertplatz.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Colin Smith on geo.hlipp.de", "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ADresden_-_Albertplatz_-_geo.hlipp.de_-_32392.jpg", "alt_en": "Albertplatz in Dresden", "alt_zh": "德累斯顿的阿尔伯特广场"} },
  { id: "de-huelshoff", country: "de", name: "Burg Hülshoff", name_zh: "许尔斯霍夫城堡", lat: 51.971621, lon: 7.503854, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "安内特·冯·德罗斯特-许尔斯霍夫 1797 年生于明斯特以西这座有护城河环绕的庄园宅邸，并在这里长大。今为纪念馆和文学中心", book_ids: ["the-jews-beech", "am-turme"], note_en: "Annette von Droste-Hülshoff was born in 1797 in this moated manor house west of Münster and grew up here. It is now a museum and a centre for literature", coord_checked: "Burg Hülshoff (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burg_H%C3%BClshoff, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-huelshoff.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 532, "author": "Günter Seggebäing", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AWasserburg_H%C3%BClshoff.JPG", "alt_en": "The moated Burg Hülshoff", "alt_zh": "护城河环绕的许尔斯霍夫城堡"} },
  { id: "de-meersburg", country: "de", name: "Meersburg Castle", name_zh: "梅尔斯堡城堡", lat: 47.693889, lon: 9.270278, place_type: "original", note: "博登湖畔高处的古堡，当年属于德罗斯特的姐夫约瑟夫·冯·拉斯贝格。她 1841 年第一次来住，随后那个冬天在这里写出了许多最好的诗，1848 年在堡中去世。她住过的房间可以参观", note_en: "The old castle above Lake Constance belonged to Droste's brother-in-law Joseph von Laßberg. She first came to stay in 1841, wrote many of her best poems here in the winter that followed, and died in the castle in 1848. Her rooms can be visited", coord_checked: "Burg Meersburg (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burg_Meersburg, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-meersburg.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 516, "author": "Carsten Steger", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AAerial_image_of_the_Burg_Meersburg_%28view_from_the_south%29.jpg", "alt_en": "Meersburg Castle from the air", "alt_zh": "空中俯瞰梅尔斯堡城堡"} },
  { id: "de-bellersen", country: "de", name: "Bellersen ('the village of B.')", name_zh: "贝勒森（“B 村”）", lat: 51.777222, lon: 9.191667, place_type: "prototype", prototype_of: "帕德博恩以东林木茂密的山丘里的村子，小说里只称“B 村”。德罗斯特的情节取自真事：1783 年一个犹太商贩在这里被杀。她是在附近伯肯多夫的外祖父母家庄园里听人讲起这件事的", prototype_of_en: "The village in the wooded hills east of Paderborn that the story calls only 'B.'. Droste took the plot from a real case: the murder of a Jewish trader here in 1783. She had heard it told on her grandparents' estate at nearby Bökendorf", coord_checked: "Bellersen (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bellersen, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-bellersen.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 508, "author": "Ath", "license": "CC0", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ABellersen_-_Meinolfusstra%C3%9Fe_7.jpg", "alt_en": "A half-timbered farmhouse in Bellersen", "alt_zh": "贝勒森的一幢桁架结构农舍"} },
  { id: "de-ribbeck", country: "de", name: "Ribbeck, Havelland", name_zh: "里贝克村，哈弗尔兰", lat: 52.624444, lon: 12.751667, place_type: "original", note: "柏林以西的村子，冯·里贝克家族的庄园就在这里。歌谣里的那棵梨树长在教堂旁老庄主的墓上，1911 年被一场风暴刮倒；树桩保存在教堂里，原处又栽了一棵新树", note_en: "The village west of Berlin where the von Ribbeck family had its manor. The pear tree of the ballad stood by the church on the old squire's grave until a storm brought it down in 1911; its stump is kept in the church, and a new tree has been planted", coord_checked: "Ribbeck (Nauen) (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ribbeck_(Nauen), 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-ribbeck.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Radler59 ( talk )", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ARibbeck_Dorfkirche-02.jpg", "alt_en": "The village church at Ribbeck", "alt_zh": "里贝克村的教堂"} },
  { id: "de-ebergoetzen", country: "de", name: "Wilhelm Busch Mill, Ebergötzen", name_zh: "威廉·布施磨坊，埃伯格岑", lat: 51.5705, lon: 10.106635, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "威廉·布施九岁时被送到这里，跟当乡村牧师的舅舅读书，从 1841 年住到 1846 年。他最要好的朋友是磨坊主的儿子埃里希·巴赫曼，两人在磨坊里外度过的童年被看作《马克斯和莫里茨》的由来。磨坊今为博物馆", book_ids: ["max-and-moritz"], note_en: "Wilhelm Busch was sent here at nine to be taught by his uncle, the village pastor, and stayed from 1841 to 1846. His closest friend was the miller's son Erich Bachmann, and their boyhood in and around the mill is taken as the seed of Max and Moritz. The mill is a museum", coord_checked: "Wilhelm-Busch-Mühle (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilhelm-Busch-M%C3%BChle, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-ebergoetzen.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 510, "author": "Jan Stubenitzky ( Dehio )", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AWilhelmBuschMuehle_02.jpg", "alt_en": "The Wilhelm Busch Mill at Ebergötzen", "alt_zh": "埃伯格岑的威廉·布施磨坊"} },
  { id: "de-radebeul", country: "de", name: "Karl May Museum, Radebeul", name_zh: "卡尔·迈博物馆，拉德博伊尔", lat: 51.102708, lon: 13.673517, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "卡尔·迈从 1896 年起住在这座“老铁手别墅”里，直到 1912 年去世。别墅和花园里的原木屋展出他的书房、藏书和北美印第安人文物。他没有到过美国西部，却把印第安人写成了几代德国读者心目中的英雄", book_ids: ["winnetou"], note_en: "Karl May lived in the 'Villa Shatterhand' from 1896 until his death in 1912. The villa and the log house in its garden show his study and library and a collection on the Indians of North America, whom he made into heroes for generations of German readers without having seen the West", coord_checked: "Karl-May-Museum (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karl-May-Museum, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-radebeul.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 796, "author": "Immanuel Giel; derivative work: Regi51", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AVilla_Shatterhand-2.jpg", "alt_en": "Villa Shatterhand in Radebeul", "alt_zh": "拉德博伊尔的“老铁手别墅”"} },
  { id: "de-naumburg-nietzsche", country: "de", name: "Nietzsche House, Naumburg", name_zh: "尼采故居，瑙姆堡", lat: 51.15004, lon: 11.81256, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "尼采的母亲 1858 年搬进这幢房子，那时他还是个中学生。精神崩溃之后，他 1890 年被接回这里，由母亲照料，直到她 1897 年去世", book_ids: ["thus-spoke-zarathustra"], note_en: "Nietzsche's mother moved into this house in 1858, when he was a schoolboy. After his collapse he was brought back to it in 1890, and she nursed him here until her death in 1897", coord_checked: "Nietzsche-Haus Naumburg (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nietzsche-Haus_Naumburg, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-naumburg-nietzsche.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "Thomas Hummel", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ANietzsche-Haus_%28Naumburg%29_01.jpg", "alt_en": "The Nietzsche House in Naumburg", "alt_zh": "瑙姆堡的尼采故居"} },
  { id: "de-lichtenstein", country: "de", name: "Lichtenstein Castle", name_zh: "利希滕施泰因城堡", lat: 48.406639, lon: 9.25825, place_type: "original", note: "豪夫 1826 年的历史小说里，被放逐的符腾堡公爵乌尔里希藏身在施瓦本山这块峭壁上的城堡附近。受这本书的启发，符腾堡的威廉伯爵 1840 年到 1842 年在旧基址上建起了今天的城堡", note_en: "Hauff's historical novel of 1826 has the outlawed Duke Ulrich of Württemberg hide near a castle on this crag of the Swabian Alb. Inspired by the book, Count Wilhelm of Württemberg had the present castle built on the old foundations between 1840 and 1842", book_ids: ["lichtenstein"], coord_checked: "Schloss Lichtenstein (Württemberg) (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schloss_Lichtenstein_(W%C3%BCrttemberg), 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-lichtenstein.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "-donald-", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ASchloss_Lichtenstein_04-2010.jpg", "alt_en": "Lichtenstein Castle on its crag", "alt_zh": "峭壁上的利希滕施泰因城堡"} },
  { id: "de-drachenfels", country: "de", name: "Drachenfels", name_zh: "龙岩山", lat: 50.665278, lon: 7.209722, place_type: "original", note: "波恩以南、莱茵河畔七峰山第一座山峰上的城堡废墟。拜伦 1816 年 5 月沿莱茵河上行时见到它，他写进《恰尔德·哈洛尔德游记》里的那首歌让这里成了莱茵河上游人最多的地方之一", note_en: "The ruined castle on the first of the Seven Hills above the Rhine, south of Bonn. Byron saw it in May 1816 on his way up the river, and the song he set into Childe Harold made it one of the most visited spots on the Rhine", coord_checked: "Drachenfels (Siebengebirge) (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drachenfels_(Siebengebirge), 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-drachenfels.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "OnwardToThePast", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_view_of_the_surrounding_mountains_of_the_Siebengebirge_from_the_top_of_the_Drachenfels_mountain_near_the_former_Burg_Drachenfels_%282022%29.jpg", "alt_en": "The Siebengebirge seen from the top of the Drachenfels", "alt_zh": "从龙岩山顶望七峰山"} },
  { id: "de-ansbach", country: "de", name: "Ansbach", name_zh: "安斯巴赫", lat: 49.300833, lon: 10.571667, place_type: "original", note: "弃儿卡斯帕尔·豪泽尔 1828 年出现在纽伦堡时几乎不会说话，1831 年起住在安斯巴赫。1833 年 12 月 14 日，他从宫廷花园带着刀伤回到家，三天后死去。瓦塞尔曼的小说一直写到这个结局", note_en: "The foundling Kaspar Hauser, who had appeared in Nuremberg in 1828 hardly able to speak, lived in Ansbach from 1831. On 14 December 1833 he came home from the court garden with a stab wound and died three days later. Wassermann's novel follows him to this end", coord_checked: "Ansbach (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ansbach, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-ansbach.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Thilo Parg", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AKaspar_Hauser_Denkmal_1.jpg", "alt_en": "The Kaspar Hauser monument in Ansbach", "alt_zh": "安斯巴赫的卡斯帕尔·豪泽尔纪念像"} },
  { id: "de-bad-woerishofen", country: "de", name: "Bad Wörishofen", name_zh: "巴特沃里斯霍芬", lat: 48.002778, lon: 10.5925, place_type: "prototype", prototype_of: "巴伐利亚的温泉小城，克奈普神父水疗法的发源地。二十岁的凯瑟琳·曼斯菲尔德 1909 年下半年住在这里，她第一本书里那些尖刻的速写，背景就是一家同她住过的那种膳宿公寓", prototype_of_en: "The Bavarian spa of Father Kneipp's water cure. Katherine Mansfield, twenty years old, spent the second half of 1909 here, and the sharp sketches of her first book are set in a boarding house like the one she stayed in", book_ids: ["in-a-german-pension"], coord_checked: "Bad Wörishofen (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bad_W%C3%B6rishofen, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-bad-woerishofen.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "Spurzem", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ABad_W%C3%B6rishofen%2C_Kurhaus_-_Eingang_%282015-08-25_2931_Sp%29.JPG", "alt_en": "The Kurhaus at Bad Wörishofen", "alt_zh": "巴特沃里斯霍芬的疗养大厅"} },
  { id: "de-ruegen", country: "de", name: "Königsstuhl, Rügen", name_zh: "国王之椅，吕根岛", lat: 54.573056, lon: 13.6625, place_type: "original", note: "亚斯蒙德半岛上的白垩悬崖，德国最大岛屿上最有名的景观。伊丽莎白·冯·阿尼姆的这部诙谐游记小说，让叙述者在夏天乘马车绕吕根岛走了一圈", note_en: "The white chalk cliff on the Jasmund peninsula, the best-known view on Germany's largest island. Elizabeth von Arnim's comic travel novel takes its narrator round the whole of Rügen by carriage in the summer", book_ids: ["elizabeth-in-rugen"], coord_checked: "Königsstuhl (Rügen) (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/K%C3%B6nigsstuhl_(R%C3%BCgen), 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-ruegen.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 484, "author": "Thomas Wolf , www.foto-tw.de", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AK%C3%B6nigsstuhl_und_Viktoria-Sicht.jpg", "alt_en": "The chalk cliffs of the Königsstuhl from the sea", "alt_zh": "从海上望国王之椅的白垩崖"} },
  { id: "de-trier-marx", country: "de", name: "Karl Marx House, Trier", name_zh: "卡尔·马克思故居，特里尔", lat: 49.753906, lon: 6.635749, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "卡尔·马克思 1818 年 5 月 5 日生于这幢房子。今为介绍他生平和著作的博物馆", book_ids: ["communist-manifesto"], note_en: "Karl Marx was born in this house on 5 May 1818. It is a museum of his life and writings", coord_checked: "Karl-Marx-Haus (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karl-Marx-Haus, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-trier-marx.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "FrDr", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AKarl-Marx-Haus_Trier_01.jpg", "alt_en": "The Karl Marx House in Trier", "alt_zh": "特里尔的卡尔·马克思故居"} },
  { id: "de-hotel-elephant", country: "de", name: "Hotel Elephant, Weimar", name_zh: "大象旅馆，魏玛", lat: 50.978889, lon: 11.330278, place_type: "original", note: "魏玛集市广场上的老旅馆。托马斯·曼的小说就从 1816 年夏洛特·凯斯特纳来到这里写起，她是歌德《维特》里的绿蒂，这时已是六十三岁的寡妇", note_en: "The old inn on Weimar's market square. Thomas Mann's novel begins with the arrival here, in 1816, of Charlotte Kestner, the Lotte of Goethe's Werther, now a widow of sixty-three", coord_checked: "Hotel Elephant (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hotel_Elephant, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-hotel-elephant.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 462, "author": "User:Mahlum", "license": "Public domain", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AHotel_Elephant_Weimar.jpg", "alt_en": "The Hotel Elephant on the market square in Weimar", "alt_zh": "魏玛集市广场上的大象旅馆"} },
  { id: "de-maulbronn", country: "de", name: "Maulbronn Monastery", name_zh: "毛尔布龙修道院", lat: 49.0011, lon: 8.8129, place_type: "prototype", prototype_of: "阿尔卑斯山以北保存最完好的中世纪西多会修道院，1556 年起成为新教神学校。黑塞 1891 年入学，1892 年 3 月逃走。《在轮下》里压垮汉斯·吉本拉特的神学校，《纳尔齐斯与歌尔德蒙》里的“玛利亚布隆”，都是这里", prototype_of_en: "The best-preserved medieval Cistercian monastery north of the Alps, a Protestant seminary since 1556. Hesse was a pupil here from 1891 and ran away in March 1892. It is the seminary that breaks Hans Giebenrath in Beneath the Wheel and the 'Mariabronn' of Narcissus and Goldmund", book_ids: ["beneath-the-wheel"], coord_checked: "Kloster Maulbronn (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kloster_Maulbronn, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-maulbronn.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 404, "author": "Elke Wetzig ( Elya )", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AKloster_Maulbronn_2009.jpg", "alt_en": "The monastery church and forecourt at Maulbronn", "alt_zh": "毛尔布龙修道院的教堂和前院"} },
  { id: "de-augsburg-brechthaus", country: "de", name: "Brecht House, Augsburg", name_zh: "布莱希特故居，奥格斯堡", lat: 48.369978, lon: 10.900085, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "贝托尔特·布莱希特 1898 年 2 月 10 日生于奥格斯堡旧手工业区两条水渠之间的这幢房子。今为纪念馆", book_ids: ["mother-courage", "life-of-galileo"], note_en: "Bertolt Brecht was born on 10 February 1898 in this house between two canals in the old craftsmen's quarter of Augsburg. It is a museum", coord_checked: "Brechthaus (Augsburg) (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brechthaus_(Augsburg), 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-augsburg-brechthaus.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 537, "author": "Gerd Eichmann", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AAugsburg-Brechthaus-04-gje.jpg", "alt_en": "The Brecht House in Augsburg", "alt_zh": "奥格斯堡的布莱希特故居"} },
  { id: "de-buckow", country: "de", name: "Brecht-Weigel House, Buckow", name_zh: "布莱希特-魏格尔故居，布科", lat: 52.567742, lon: 14.063033, place_type: "original", note: "从 1952 年起，布莱希特和海伦妮·魏格尔每年夏天住在柏林以东舍尔米策尔湖边的这幢房子里。1953 年夏天，他在这里写下了《布科哀歌》里的那些短诗", note_en: "From 1952 Brecht and Helene Weigel spent their summers in this house on the Schermützelsee, east of Berlin. He wrote the short poems of the Buckow Elegies here in the summer of 1953", coord_checked: "Brecht-Weigel-Haus (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brecht-Weigel-Haus, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-buckow.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 652, "author": "Lienhard Schulz", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ABrecht-Weigel-Haus_Buckow_01.jpg", "alt_en": "The Brecht-Weigel House in Buckow", "alt_zh": "布科的布莱希特-魏格尔故居"} },
  { id: "de-dorotheenstadt", country: "de", name: "Dorotheenstadt Cemetery, Berlin", name_zh: "多罗特娅城公墓，柏林", lat: 52.528411, lon: 13.383704, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "肖塞大街上的小公墓。布莱希特和海伦妮·魏格尔葬在这里，隔壁就是他们住过的房子。黑格尔和费希特也葬在这里，还有亨利希·曼、安娜·西格斯和克里斯塔·沃尔夫", book_ids: ["mother-courage", "the-seventh-cross", "divided-heaven"], note_en: "The small cemetery on Chausseestraße where Brecht and Helene Weigel are buried, next door to the house they lived in. Hegel and Fichte lie here, and so do Heinrich Mann, Anna Seghers and Christa Wolf", coord_checked: "Friedhof der Dorotheenstädtischen und Friedrichswerderschen Gemeinden (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedhof_der_Dorotheenst%C3%A4dtischen_und_Friedrichswerderschen_Gemeinden, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-dorotheenstadt.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "User:W.pseudon", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AGrab-Brecht-Weigel_%282%29.JPG", "alt_en": "The gravestones of Bertolt Brecht and Helene Weigel", "alt_zh": "布莱希特和海伦妮·魏格尔的墓石"} },
  { id: "de-rheinsberg", country: "de", name: "Rheinsberg Palace", name_zh: "莱茵斯贝格宫", lat: 53.098611, lon: 12.889444, place_type: "original", note: "柏林以北的湖畔宫殿，腓特烈大帝做王储时在这里度过了他自称最幸福的几年。图霍尔斯基的成名作让一对柏林的年轻恋人到这里过了一个周末。宫里今设有图霍尔斯基文学博物馆", note_en: "The lakeside palace north of Berlin where Frederick the Great spent what he called his happiest years as crown prince. Tucholsky's first success sends two young lovers from Berlin here for a weekend. The palace now houses a Tucholsky museum", coord_checked: "Schloss Rheinsberg (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schloss_Rheinsberg, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-rheinsberg.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 534, "author": "JoachimKohler-HB", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ASchloss_Rheinsberg_-_20160626.jpg", "alt_en": "Rheinsberg Palace across the water", "alt_zh": "隔水望莱茵斯贝格宫"} },
  { id: "de-gendarmenmarkt", country: "de", name: "Schauspielhaus (Konzerthaus), Gendarmenmarkt, Berlin", name_zh: "御林广场剧院（今音乐厅），柏林", lat: 52.513611, lon: 13.392222, place_type: "original", note: "申克尔设计的御林广场剧院当年是普鲁士国家剧院，演员古斯塔夫·格林德根斯 1934 年起在戈林的庇护下担任院长。曾是他内弟的克劳斯·曼把这段发迹史写成了小说", note_en: "Schinkel's theatre on the Gendarmenmarkt was the Prussian State Theatre, which the actor Gustaf Gründgens directed from 1934 under Göring's protection. Klaus Mann, once his brother-in-law, wrote the career into a novel", coord_checked: "Schauspielhaus (Berlin) (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schauspielhaus_(Berlin), 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-gendarmenmarkt.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 431, "author": "Ansgar Koreng", "license": "CC BY 3.0 de", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/de/deed.en", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A150418_Konzerthaus_Berlin.jpg", "alt_en": "The Konzerthaus on the Gendarmenmarkt", "alt_zh": "御林广场上的音乐厅"} },
  { id: "de-bebelplatz", country: "de", name: "Bebelplatz (book-burning memorial), Berlin", name_zh: "倍倍尔广场（焚书纪念地），柏林", lat: 52.516389, lon: 13.393889, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "1933 年 5 月 10 日夜里，大学生们在这个广场上烧掉了约两万册书。埃里希·凯斯特纳站在人群里，看着自己的书被扔进火堆。如今地面上有一扇玻璃窗，往下看是一间摆着空白书架的白色房间", book_ids: ["fabian-kaestner", "emil-and-the-detectives", "all-quiet-on-the-western-front", "book-of-songs"], note_en: "On the night of 10 May 1933 students burned some twenty thousand books on this square. Erich Kästner stood in the crowd and watched his own go into the fire. A window in the paving looks down into a room of empty white shelves", coord_checked: "Bebelplatz (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bebelplatz, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-bebelplatz.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 619, "author": "Kemmi.1 (Stefan Kemmerling)", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Empty_Library_-_Bebelplatz_mit_Mahnmal_B%C3%BCcherverbrennung_Aug_2009.jpg", "alt_en": "The 'Empty Library' memorial set into Bebelplatz", "alt_zh": "嵌在倍倍尔广场地面上的“空书架”纪念装置"} },
  { id: "de-bahnhof-zoo", country: "de", name: "Bahnhof Zoo, Berlin", name_zh: "动物园火车站，柏林", lat: 52.5072, lon: 13.3325, place_type: "original", note: "柏林墙年代西柏林的主要火车站。1970 年代，车站背后一带是城里吸食海洛因的青少年聚集的地方，这本书让外界知道了这个世界", note_en: "West Berlin's main station in the years of the Wall. In the 1970s the area behind it was the city's meeting place for young heroin users, the world this book made known", coord_checked: "Bahnhof Berlin Zoologischer Garten (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bahnhof_Berlin_Zoologischer_Garten, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-bahnhof-zoo.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 500, "author": "Wolfgang Pehlemann Wiesbaden Germany", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0 de", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/de/deed.en", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ABerlin_Bahnhof_Zoo_Zoologischer_Garten_2007.jpg", "alt_en": "Bahnhof Zoologischer Garten", "alt_zh": "动物园火车站"} },
  { id: "de-sonnenallee", country: "de", name: "Sonnenallee, Berlin", name_zh: "太阳大道，柏林", lat: 52.474939, lon: 13.4536, place_type: "original", note: "一条将近五公里长的大街，柏林墙只把最后几百米留在了东柏林，截断处设有一个过境口岸。布鲁西希的小说写的就是 1970 年代这短短的一头", note_en: "A street nearly five kilometres long, of which the Wall left only the last few hundred metres in East Berlin, with a border crossing at the cut. Brussig's novel is set on that short end in the 1970s", coord_checked: "Sonnenallee (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonnenallee, 2026-11-23" },
  { id: "de-landungsbruecken", country: "de", name: "St. Pauli Landungsbrücken, Hamburg", name_zh: "圣保利栈桥，汉堡", lat: 53.546111, lon: 9.966667, place_type: "original", note: "易北河圣保利一段的浮码头。博尔谢特的这部剧是 1947 年用几天时间写成的，同年他去世，年仅二十六岁；戏就从这里的一座浮桥上开场", note_en: "The floating landing stages on the Elbe at St. Pauli. Borchert's play, written in a few days in 1947, the year he died at twenty-six, begins on a pontoon here", coord_checked: "St. Pauli-Landungsbrücken (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Pauli-Landungsbr%C3%BCcken, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-landungsbruecken.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 500, "author": "Dietmar Rabich", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AHamburg%2C_Landungsbr%C3%BCcken_--_2016_--_3173.jpg", "alt_en": "The St. Pauli Landungsbrücken and the harbour", "alt_zh": "圣保利栈桥和港口"} },
  { id: "de-seebuell", country: "de", name: "Nolde's House, Seebüll", name_zh: "诺尔德故居，泽比尔", lat: 54.884056, lon: 8.773611, place_type: "prototype", prototype_of: "画家埃米尔·诺尔德在靠近丹麦边境的沼泽地土丘上建的房子和花园。诺尔德 1941 年被禁止作画，伦茨小说里的画家南森显然以他为原型", prototype_of_en: "The house and garden the painter Emil Nolde built on a mound in the marshes by the Danish border. Nolde, forbidden to paint in 1941, is the evident model for the painter Nansen in Lenz's novel", coord_checked: "Nolde Stiftung Seebüll (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nolde_Stiftung_Seeb%C3%BCll, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-seebuell.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "FaceMePLS from The Hague, The Netherlands", "license": "CC BY 2.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ANolde_Museum_-_Seeb%C3%BCll_%2824543520598%29.jpg", "alt_en": "Nolde's house on its mound at Seebüll", "alt_zh": "泽比尔土丘上的诺尔德故居"} },
  { id: "de-kluetz", country: "de", name: "Klütz ('Jerichow')", name_zh: "克吕茨（“耶里肖”）", lat: 53.966667, lon: 11.166667, place_type: "prototype", prototype_of: "梅克伦堡西北部靠近波罗的海的小城，读者认为它是约翰逊虚构的耶里肖的原型。2006 年，以他名字命名的文学之家在这里的一座旧粮仓里开放", prototype_of_en: "The small town near the Baltic in north-west Mecklenburg that readers take for the model of Johnson's invented Jerichow. A literature house named after him opened in an old granary here in 2006", coord_checked: "Klütz (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kl%C3%BCtz, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-kluetz.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "Concord", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AKluetz_Literaturhaus_Uwe_Johnson.JPG", "alt_en": "The Uwe Johnson Literature House in Klütz", "alt_zh": "克吕茨的乌韦·约翰逊文学之家"} },
  { id: "de-hiddensee", country: "de", name: "Gerhart Hauptmann House, Hiddensee", name_zh: "盖哈特·霍普特曼故居，希登塞岛", lat: 54.585194, lon: 13.103778, place_type: "original", note: "吕根岛以西的无车小岛。霍普特曼 1930 年买下克洛斯特村的这幢消夏别墅，死后葬在岛上的教堂墓地。东德时期，这座岛吸引着一批在旅馆饭店打季节工的“出走者”，卢茨·赛勒的小说写的就是他们", note_en: "The car-free island west of Rügen. Hauptmann bought this summer house at Kloster in 1930 and is buried in the island churchyard. Under the GDR the island drew dropouts who worked the season in its inns, the world of Lutz Seiler's novel", book_ids: ["pl-weber"], coord_checked: "Gerhart-Hauptmann-Museum (Hiddensee) (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gerhart-Hauptmann-Museum_(Hiddensee), 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-hiddensee.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 606, "author": "Nikater", "license": "Public domain", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AKloster%2C_Gerhart_Hauptmanns_Haus02.jpg", "alt_en": "Gerhart Hauptmann's house at Kloster on Hiddensee", "alt_zh": "希登塞岛克洛斯特村的霍普特曼故居"} },
  { id: "de-telgte", country: "de", name: "Telgte", name_zh: "特尔格特", lat: 51.98428, lon: 7.78564, place_type: "original", note: "明斯特以东埃姆斯河畔的朝圣小城；1647 年，威斯特伐利亚和约正在明斯特谈判。格拉斯把一场虚构的当时德语诗人的聚会安排在这里", note_en: "A small pilgrimage town on the Ems, east of Münster, where the Peace of Westphalia was being negotiated in 1647. Grass sets an invented gathering of the German poets of the day here", coord_checked: "Telgte (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telgte, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-telgte.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 565, "author": "Hangsurfer", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AMarienkapelle_und_Propstei.jpg", "alt_en": "The pilgrimage chapel in Telgte", "alt_zh": "特尔格特的朝圣小教堂"} },
  { id: "de-scharmuetzelsee", country: "de", name: "Scharmützelsee", name_zh: "沙尔米策尔湖", lat: 52.25, lon: 14.05, place_type: "original", note: "柏林东南的狭长湖泊。埃彭贝克的小说写的是湖边一幢消夏别墅的故事（原型之一是她祖父母的房子），以及二十世纪里在这幢房子住过又失去它的人们", note_en: "The long lake south-east of Berlin. Erpenbeck's novel is the story of one summer house on its shore, her own grandparents' house among its models, and of those who lived in it and lost it across the twentieth century", coord_checked: "Scharmützelsee (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scharm%C3%BCtzelsee, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-scharmuetzelsee.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "Meine Mutter (Erlaubnis liegt vor)", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A2013-06-30_Scharm%C3%BCtzelsee_01.jpg", "alt_en": "The Scharmützelsee", "alt_zh": "沙尔米策尔湖"} },
  { id: "de-halle", country: "de", name: "Halle (Saale)", name_zh: "萨勒河畔哈雷", lat: 51.482798, lon: 11.969735, place_type: "original", note: "工业城市。克里斯塔·沃尔夫 1959 年到 1962 年住在这里，还在一家车辆厂工作过一段时间。她那部写一对恋人被柏林墙分开的小说，背景就是这座城", note_en: "The industrial city where Christa Wolf lived from 1959 to 1962 and worked for a time in a railway-carriage factory. Her novel of a couple parted by the Wall is set here", coord_checked: "Marktplatz (Halle (Saale)) (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marktplatz_(Halle_(Saale)), 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-halle.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 533, "author": "paul muster", "license": "CC BY 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AH%C3%A4ndel_und_seine_5_T%C3%BCrme_-_Marktplatz_Halle_%28Saale%29_-_Roter_Turm_Marktkirche_-_panoramio.jpg", "alt_en": "The market square of Halle with the Handel monument and its towers, in silhouette", "alt_zh": "哈雷集市广场上亨德尔像和塔楼的剪影"} },
  { id: "de-runde-ecke", country: "de", name: "'Runde Ecke' Stasi Museum, Leipzig", name_zh: "“圆角楼”斯塔西博物馆，莱比锡", lat: 51.341742, lon: 12.370048, place_type: "original", note: "斯塔西的莱比锡地区总部。1989 年 12 月 4 日被示威者占领，后来辟为博物馆，办公室大体保持着当时的样子。安娜·方德采访的人里有好几位是莱比锡人", note_en: "The Leipzig district headquarters of the Stasi, occupied by demonstrators on 4 December 1989 and kept as a museum with its offices much as they were left. Several of the people Anna Funder talks to are from Leipzig", coord_checked: "Gedenkstätte Museum in der „Runden Ecke“ (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gedenkst%C3%A4tte_Museum_in_der_%E2%80%9ERunden_Ecke%E2%80%9C, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-runde-ecke.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 527, "author": "Bettenburg", "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0 de", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0/de/deed.en", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ARunde-Ecke-Leipzig.jpg", "alt_en": "The 'Runde Ecke' in Leipzig", "alt_zh": "莱比锡的“圆角楼”"} },
  { id: "de-buchenwald", country: "de", name: "Buchenwald Memorial", name_zh: "布痕瓦尔德纪念馆", lat: 51.022143, lon: 11.249046, place_type: "original", note: "魏玛上方埃特斯山上的集中营，1937 年到 1945 年间约有五万六千人死在这里。布鲁诺·阿皮茨在这里被关了八年，他的小说写的是集中营最后的几个星期", note_en: "The concentration camp on the Ettersberg above Weimar, where some 56,000 people died between 1937 and 1945. Bruno Apitz was a prisoner here for eight years and set his novel in the camp's last weeks", coord_checked: "KZ Buchenwald (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/KZ_Buchenwald, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-buchenwald.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Diogo P. Duarte", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ABuchenwald-KZ_Jedem_das_Seine_Tor_Nebel_2007.JPG", "alt_en": "The gatehouse of Buchenwald in fog", "alt_zh": "雾中的布痕瓦尔德营门"} },
  { id: "de-odeonsplatz", country: "de", name: "Odeonsplatz, Munich", name_zh: "音乐厅广场，慕尼黑", lat: 48.143571, lon: 11.578029, place_type: "original", note: "路德维希大街尽头的广场，旁边是统帅堂和铁阿提纳教堂。托马斯·曼 1902 年的这篇小说，开头就是穿过这座光彩照人、为艺术而疯狂的城市向它走去", note_en: "The square at the foot of Ludwigstraße, with the Feldherrnhalle and the Theatine church. Thomas Mann's story of 1902 opens with a walk through the radiant, art-mad city towards it", coord_checked: "Odeonsplatz (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odeonsplatz, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-odeonsplatz.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 516, "author": "Thomas Wolf , www.foto-tw.de", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0 de", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/de/deed.en", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AFeldherrnhalle_M%C3%BCnchen.jpg", "alt_en": "The Feldherrnhalle on Odeonsplatz", "alt_zh": "音乐厅广场上的统帅堂"} },
  { id: "de-lmu-lichthof", country: "de", name: "White Rose Memorial, University of Munich", name_zh: "白玫瑰纪念地，慕尼黑大学", lat: 48.150833, lon: 11.580278, place_type: "pilgrimage", note: "1943 年 2 月 18 日，汉斯·朔尔和索菲·朔尔把小组的第六份传单放在各个教室门口，又把剩下的几份从楼上撒进主楼的中庭。一名校工看见了他们；四天后两人被处决。中庭旁设有纪念室", book_ids: ["white-rose-leaflets"], note_en: "On 18 February 1943 Hans and Sophie Scholl laid the group's sixth leaflet outside the lecture rooms and threw the last copies down into the atrium of the main building. A caretaker saw them; four days later they were executed. A memorial room is beside the atrium", coord_checked: "Hauptgebäude der Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hauptgeb%C3%A4ude_der_Ludwig-Maximilians-Universit%C3%A4t_M%C3%BCnchen, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-lmu-lichthof.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Frupa", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ALMU_Lichthof_2025_quer.jpg", "alt_en": "The atrium of the main building of Munich University", "alt_zh": "慕尼黑大学主楼的中庭"} },
  { id: "de-wertach", country: "de", name: "Wertach ('W.')", name_zh: "韦尔塔赫（“W 村”）", lat: 47.603333, lon: 10.408889, place_type: "prototype", prototype_of: "阿尔高地区的村子，W. G. 塞巴尔德 1944 年生于此，一直住到八岁。《眩晕》最后一部分里，叙述者步行回去的那个“W 村”就是这里", prototype_of_en: "The village in the Allgäu where W. G. Sebald was born in 1944 and lived until he was eight. It is the 'W.' to which the narrator of Vertigo walks back in the book's last part", coord_checked: "Wertach (German Wikipedia, article coordinates) — https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wertach, 2026-11-23", photo: {"file": "images/places/de-wertach.jpg", "width": 800, "height": 600, "author": "Flodur63", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0", "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AWertach%2C_OA_-_Auf_der_Bl%C3%B6%C3%9Fe_-_Oberellegg%2C_Vorderschneid.jpg", "alt_en": "Winter hills at Wertach in the Allgäu", "alt_zh": "阿尔高地区韦尔塔赫的冬日山丘"} }
